use std.stringbuilder.StringBuilder

/// How many characters this string is.
///
/// Characters and not bytes: a two-byte character counts one here and two in
/// [`byteLength`]. That difference is the whole of the line between them, and
/// it is why this walks the text where `byteLength` reads a header word. The
/// walk is [`charactersBefore`], whose bound here is the whole string.
///
/// It was `chars().count()` in Rust, one `IntrinsicCall` per call and the
/// most-called one in the repository. ADR 0064 moves it here: a character
/// count is a policy over a representation, not an operation of the machine,
/// and the bound below it is `core.byteLength` — a header load — with one run
/// load a character over it. A loop the native tier compiles, in place of a
/// call it can only cross back out of.
export fn length(text: String) -> Int {
  charactersBefore(text, core.byteLength(text))
}

/// How many characters begin before byte `end` of `text`.
///
/// The one UTF-8 walk in this file, under [`length`] and [`indexOf`]. Both
/// ask the same question of the same bytes and differ only in where they stop
/// — the whole string, and the offset a search answered — so one body takes a
/// bound and the callers supply it.
///
/// A `String` is valid UTF-8 in its shortest form — nothing makes one from
/// anything else — so the lead byte alone says how wide the character it
/// begins is, and the continuation bytes after it need not be looked at at
/// all: below 128 is one byte, below 224 is two, below 240 is three, and
/// anything left is four. A lead byte of `11111xxx` cannot occur, so the last
/// case needs no test of its own — [`codePointAtByte`]'s reasoning, used here
/// to skip a character rather than to decode one. The loop advances by the
/// width it read, so it lands on a lead byte every time and never asks a
/// continuation byte how wide it is.
///
/// **`end` must begin a character**, or be the end of the string. Both
/// callers' do: one is the byte length, and the other is where a needle's
/// bytes were found, which [`indexOf`] argues is a boundary. Nothing checks
/// it — a bound inside a character would make the walk step past it and count
/// on, which is a wrong answer rather than an unsound read, and this is a
/// private body with two callers rather than an API.
///
/// **The factoring was measured rather than assumed**, by the rule the
/// [`startsWith`] migration set: it is taken only if the lowered instruction
/// sequence at the existing caller's sites is unchanged. It is. At
/// `covefmt.column` — 137,904 calls over this repository's 274 `.cove` files,
/// and three inlined copies of this loop — every program counter holds the
/// same opcode with the same execution count before and after, and only the
/// frame slots the temporaries land in are renumbered; the function's total
/// is 31,232,910 instructions either way. Whole programs say the same: covefmt
/// is 831,921,093 instructions, 4,098,232 allocations and 51,338,466 allocated
/// words on both sides, and cq 264,229,256 and 272,100,121 fuel on both. The
/// inliner expands this into [`length`] and [`length`] into its callers, so
/// the shared body costs a call at no site at all. `matchesAt` was refused in
/// the [`startsWith`] round on the same rule, for +2,178 instructions at
/// covefmt's one `endsWith` site — the rule is what differs between the two
/// answers, not the taste.
fn charactersBefore(text: String, end: Int) -> Int {
  var at = 0
  var count = 0
  while at < end {
    let lead = text.byteAt(at)
    if lead < 128 {
      at += 1
    } else if lead < 224 {
      at += 2
    } else if lead < 240 {
      at += 3
    } else {
      at += 4
    }
    count += 1
  }
  count
}

/// Whether this string is the empty one.
///
/// `core.byteLength` rather than [`length`]: a string has no characters
/// exactly when it has no bytes, because every character is at least one byte
/// and no character is none. So the question is one header load, where
/// `length() == 0` would walk the whole text to answer something its first
/// byte already settles — which it would not have done while `length` was a
/// primitive, and does now that ADR 0064 has made it a loop.
export fn isEmpty(text: String) -> Bool {
  core.byteLength(text) == 0
}

/// Whether this string begins with `prefix`.
///
/// The comparison is of **bytes**: the first `prefix.byteLength()` bytes of
/// the text against the whole of the prefix, one byte at a time, stopping at
/// the first pair that differs. A prefix longer than the text is answered on
/// the two lengths alone, before a byte of either is read.
///
/// Nothing here asks whether the comparison began or ended at a character
/// boundary, and for a *prefix* that needs less argument than [`endsWith`]
/// does — it needs none of the self-synchronization half at all. The
/// comparison starts at offset 0, and 0 is a character boundary in every
/// string there is, empty or not: no argument, a fact about where a string
/// starts. What is left is the far end, and the prefix settles it. A `String`
/// is valid UTF-8 in its shortest form, so the prefix's own bytes are whole
/// characters and end at a boundary of its own; if the text's first `m` bytes
/// are those bytes, then reading the text from 0 takes the same lead bytes and
/// the same widths and lands on exactly the same offset, so byte `m` of the
/// text begins a character too. A byte match is therefore a character match at
/// both ends, and a character-by-character comparison would find exactly what
/// this finds.
///
/// [`endsWith`] has the harder half of the same argument: its comparison
/// starts at `n - m`, which is no boundary by construction, and what makes it
/// one is that a continuation byte never begins a character. Read the two
/// together — this one is what that one would be if the offset were free.
///
/// An empty prefix answers `true`, because the loop does not run — which is
/// what `str::starts_with("")` answers, and
/// `tests/e2e/values_string_starts_with` asserts it rather than leaving it to
/// be obvious.
///
/// It was `str::starts_with` in Rust, one `IntrinsicCall` per call. ADR 0064
/// moves it here: `startsWith` is a method's name and would have to be renamed
/// the day the method was, which is Decision 2's test, and what is left
/// underneath is `core.byteLength` — a header load — with one run load a byte
/// over it. A loop the native tier compiles, in place of a call it can only
/// cross back out of.
///
/// A private `matchesAt(text, at, needle)` under both this and [`endsWith`]
/// was measured rather than assumed, and refused: the two differ only in the
/// offset, but the shared callee recomputes the needle's length that the
/// caller already took for the refusal, and its return adds a join. At
/// covefmt's one `endsWith` site that is 2,178 instructions over 1,174 calls
/// — 98,073 to 100,251 — for one body instead of two. A factoring that costs
/// its existing caller is not one.
///
/// Each question is an `if` of its own and the answer is a `return`, for the
/// reasons [`sliceBytes`] gives.
export fn startsWith(text: String, prefix: String) -> Bool {
  let prefixLength = core.byteLength(prefix)
  let textLength = core.byteLength(text)
  if prefixLength > textLength {
    return false
  }
  var at = 0
  while at < prefixLength {
    if text.byteAt(at) != prefix.byteAt(at) {
      return false
    }
    at += 1
  }
  return true
}

/// Whether this string ends with `suffix`.
///
/// The comparison is of **bytes**: the last `suffix.byteLength()` bytes of the
/// text against the whole of the suffix, one byte at a time, stopping at the
/// first pair that differs. A suffix longer than the text is answered on the
/// two lengths alone, before a byte of either is read.
///
/// Nothing here asks whether the offset the comparison starts at begins a
/// character, and that is not an omission — it is the reason a byte loop is
/// allowed to answer a question about text at all. **UTF-8 is
/// self-synchronizing.** A continuation byte is `10xxxxxx`, 128 through 191,
/// and no character begins with one; a `String` is valid UTF-8 in its shortest
/// form — nothing makes one from anything else — so a suffix's first byte is a
/// lead byte and never a continuation byte. If every byte from `n - m` onwards
/// matches, then in particular the byte at `n - m` equals the suffix's first
/// byte, so it is a lead byte too, and a lead byte in valid UTF-8 is the start
/// of a character. A byte match at `n - m` is therefore a match at a character
/// boundary by construction, and a character-by-character comparison would
/// find exactly what this finds. That is [`codePointAtByte`]'s fact read in
/// the other direction: there it says the lead byte alone decides a
/// character's width, here it says a match cannot land inside one.
///
/// An empty suffix answers `true`, because the loop does not run — which is
/// what `str::ends_with("")` answers, and `tests/e2e/values_string_ends_with`
/// asserts it rather than leaving it to be obvious.
///
/// It was `str::ends_with` in Rust, one `IntrinsicCall` per call. ADR 0064
/// moves it here: `endsWith` is a method's name and would have to be renamed
/// the day the method was, which is Decision 2's test, and what is left
/// underneath is `core.byteLength` — a header load — with one run load a byte
/// over it. A loop the native tier compiles, in place of a call it can only
/// cross back out of.
///
/// Each question is an `if` of its own and the answer is a `return`, for the
/// reasons [`sliceBytes`] gives.
export fn endsWith(text: String, suffix: String) -> Bool {
  let suffixLength = core.byteLength(suffix)
  let textLength = core.byteLength(text)
  if suffixLength > textLength {
    return false
  }
  let start = textLength - suffixLength
  var at = 0
  while at < suffixLength {
    if text.byteAt(start + at) != suffix.byteAt(at) {
      return false
    }
    at += 1
  }
  return true
}

/// Whether `needle` occurs anywhere in this string.
///
/// One `core.stringFind`, which is `Inst::RunFind` — a bounded search over a
/// run of packed bytes — and a comparison of its answer against -1. The
/// instruction answers the first byte offset at or after 0 where the needle's
/// bytes occur, or -1 for a needle that does not occur; an empty needle
/// occurs at 0, and a needle longer than the text does not occur at all, both
/// answered before a byte of either is read.
///
/// **It is a match of bytes, and that it is also a match of characters is the
/// argument this body owes.** It is [`endsWith`]' argument at every offset
/// rather than at one. UTF-8 is self-synchronizing: a continuation byte is
/// `10xxxxxx`, 128 through 191, and no character begins with one, while a
/// `String` is valid UTF-8 in its shortest form and nothing makes one from
/// anything else. So the needle's own first byte is a lead byte. If the
/// needle's bytes appear at offset `i` of the text, the byte at `i` equals
/// that lead byte and so is one, and a lead byte in valid UTF-8 begins a
/// character — the match starts at a character boundary, whatever `i` is. The
/// far end is [`startsWith`]' half of the same argument: the needle's bytes
/// are whole characters and end at a boundary of its own, so reading the text
/// from `i` takes the same lead bytes and the same widths and lands on `i + m`
/// — a boundary too. A byte match is a character match at both ends, and a
/// character-by-character search would find exactly what this finds.
///
/// The converse also holds and is what makes the answer complete rather than
/// merely sound: every character match is a byte match, since the characters'
/// encodings are the bytes. So neither a false positive nor a false negative
/// is possible, and no boundary check is needed anywhere.
///
/// **Why this one has an instruction underneath it when the other two do
/// not.** [`startsWith`] and [`endsWith`] compare at an offset their own
/// argument bounds — covefmt's `endsWith` reads 2,209 bytes over 1,159 calls,
/// because three quarters of them end after one byte — so a dispatch per unit
/// of that costs little, and both are Cove loops over `byteAt`. `contains`
/// must touch a haystack the caller did not size, and a Cove loop pays a VM
/// dispatch per unit of proportional work where an instruction pays one for
/// the whole of it. That is ADR 0065's rule and its measurement: seven
/// instructions a byte in Cove against one instruction for a search of any
/// length. It is not a claim about complexity — a Cove KMP would be `O(n + m)`
/// too, and would still turn its loop once a byte.
///
/// It was `str::contains` in Rust reached through one `IntrinsicCall`, which
/// is what ADR 0064's Decision 2 refuses: `contains` is a method's name and
/// would have to be renamed the day the method was. What is underneath it now
/// is named for the machine — find a byte run inside a byte run from an offset
/// — and answers a byte offset that this body turns into the `Bool` its API
/// promises, while `indexOf` will turn the same answer into a character
/// position.
///
/// Each question is an `if` of its own and the answer is a `return`, for the
/// reasons [`sliceBytes`] gives.
export fn contains(text: String, needle: String) -> Bool {
  if core.stringFind(text, needle, 0) < 0 {
    return false
  }
  return true
}

/// Where `needle` first occurs in this string, in **characters**, or `None`
/// when it does not occur.
///
/// One `core.stringFind` — the same `Inst::RunFind` [`contains`] stands on,
/// and the same argument for why a search is the one loop that stays below
/// the standard library — and then a walk of the text in front of what it
/// found. The instruction answers a **byte** offset and this answers a
/// **character** position, because that is the unit `length`, `slice` and
/// `chars` all count in and a string's API may not deal in two.
///
/// That a byte match is a character match, at whatever offset, is
/// [`contains`]' argument and is made in full there: UTF-8 is
/// self-synchronizing, so the needle's own first byte is a lead byte and the
/// byte the match lands on equals it; the needle's bytes are whole characters,
/// so the far end is a boundary too. Nothing below needs it again — what is
/// left here is only counting the characters before a boundary already known
/// to be one.
///
/// **The count walks the prefix's bytes where they are.** It is
/// [`charactersBefore`], [`length`]'s own loop over a shorter run: each lead
/// byte read and the width that byte declares stepped over. It builds
/// nothing — a `sliceBytes(0, at)` whose characters were then counted would
/// allocate a whole second copy of the prefix to answer a question about bytes
/// the receiver already holds, on the path a scanner takes most often.
///
/// A needle that is nowhere answers `None` before a byte is walked, and an
/// empty needle occurs at byte 0, so it answers `Some(0)` — which is what
/// `"".find("")` and `"abc".find("")` answer, and what `str::find` answers in
/// both. `tests/e2e/values_string_index_of` asserts all three rather than
/// leaving them to be obvious.
///
/// **That the walk is shared with [`length`] was measured**, by the rule the
/// [`startsWith`] migration set and against which that round refused
/// `matchesAt`: a factoring is taken only if the lowered sequence at the
/// existing caller's sites is unchanged. [`charactersBefore`] records the
/// measurement — it is unchanged, opcode for opcode and count for count.
///
/// It was `str::find` in Rust, with `chars().count()` over the prefix, reached
/// through one `IntrinsicCall`. ADR 0064 moves it here for Decision 2's
/// reason: `indexOf` is a method's name and would have to be renamed the day
/// the method was. What is underneath it now is named for the machine — find
/// a byte run inside a byte run from an offset — and the conversion from what
/// the machine found to what the API promises is policy, so it is Cove.
///
/// Each question is an `if` of its own and the answer is a `return`, for the
/// reasons [`sliceBytes`] gives.
export fn indexOf(text: String, needle: String) -> Option<Int> {
  let found = core.stringFind(text, needle, 0)
  if found < 0 {
    return None
  }
  return Some(charactersBefore(text, found))
}

/// How many bytes this string's UTF-8 encoding is.
///
/// Bytes and not characters: a two-byte character counts two here and one in
/// `length()`. It is the range `byteAt`, `codePointAtByte` and `sliceBytes`
/// take their offsets in, and what a `StringBuilder` copies when it appends
/// the whole of a string.
///
/// The count is the header of the object that holds the bytes, which is a fact
/// about representation this file cannot state — so the body is one core
/// intrinsic and nothing else, and the lowering expands it wherever it is
/// called. See ADR 0058.
export fn byteLength(text: String) -> Int {
  core.byteLength(text)
}

/// The Unicode scalar value of the character that begins at byte `offset`, or
/// `None` when no character begins there.
///
/// Before the start, at or past the end, and inside a character are one answer
/// on purpose: a scanner that advances by the width of what it read reaches
/// none of them, and telling them apart would cost a `Result` on the one path
/// this exists for.
///
/// A `String` is valid UTF-8 in its shortest form — nothing makes one from
/// anything else — so the lead byte alone says how wide the character is and
/// every byte after it is a continuation byte that is there: `10xxxxxx`, whose
/// low six bits are the next six bits of the scalar. That is why this reads
/// only as many bytes as the character has, and checks neither that they are
/// continuation bytes nor that the string is long enough to hold them; a lead
/// byte of `11111xxx` cannot occur, so the last case needs no test of its own.
///
/// It was a Rust decode in each evaluator. ADR 0058 (#378, Q5.9) moved it here
/// over `byteAt`, which is one run load, and each question is an `if` of its own
/// with a `return` for the reason [`sliceBytes`] gives. ASCII, the case a lexer
/// is almost always in, is decided after one load and two comparisons.
export fn codePointAtByte(text: String, offset: Int) -> Option<Int> {
  if offset < 0 {
    return None
  }
  if offset >= core.byteLength(text) {
    return None
  }
  let lead = text.byteAt(offset)
  if lead < 128 {
    return Some(lead)
  }
  // A continuation byte, 128 through 191, is the inside of a character.
  if lead < 192 {
    return None
  }
  let second = text.byteAt(offset + 1) - 128
  if lead < 224 {
    return Some((lead - 192) * 64 + second)
  }
  let third = text.byteAt(offset + 2) - 128
  if lead < 240 {
    return Some(((lead - 224) * 64 + second) * 64 + third)
  }
  let fourth = text.byteAt(offset + 3) - 128
  return Some((((lead - 240) * 64 + second) * 64 + third) * 64 + fourth)
}

/// The one-character `String` the Unicode code point `point` names, or which
/// of the two ways it names no character.
///
/// [`codePointAtByte`] run backwards, and written directly beneath it on
/// purpose: that body multiplies by 64 and adds, this one divides by 64 and
/// subtracts the multiple back off, and both ask the same four width
/// questions in the same order. A character in Cove is a `String` of length 1
/// — there is no `Character` type, which is what `chars()` already answers an
/// array of — so a code point has nothing to become but a string, and this is
/// the only operation that makes one out of a number.
///
/// It was `u32::try_from`, `char::from_u32` and `char::to_string` in Rust, one
/// `IntrinsicCall` per call. ADR 0064 moves it here for Decision 2's reason:
/// the range of Unicode and the hole in the middle of it are a **policy over a
/// representation**, not an operation of the machine, and nothing below the
/// standard library should have to know that a surrogate half is refused. What
/// is left below is the substrate ADR 0062 decides — `ensure`, `store`,
/// `commit` and a validating `finish`, reached through [`StringBuilder`]'s
/// `appendByte` — and that substrate is the whole of what this needed.
///
/// **It is the first migration that builds a string rather than reading one.**
/// Every one before it stood on a run slice or a run load; there was nothing
/// for this to stand on until `std.stringbuilder` existed, and there is nothing
/// new under it now.
///
/// # It allocates twice where the intrinsic allocated once, and the second
/// one is the substrate's rather than this body's
///
/// Measured on `benches/frompoint`: **2 allocations and 6 allocated words per
/// call, against the intrinsic's 1 and 2**, at every width and on both
/// refusals alike. ADR 0064's Decision 8 asks that this be found and stated
/// rather than discovered later, so here it is, with what it is and what it is
/// not.
///
/// It is not a capacity that was guessed too small: `withCapacity` is called
/// with the width this code point encodes to, nothing grows, and `finish`
/// hands the spare tail back — and it would not grow at any of the four
/// widths whatever number were passed, because `Machine::alloc_buffer` raises
/// a capacity to a floor of sixteen bytes and every answer here is at most
/// four. So the four `withCapacity` arguments below buy nothing measurable
/// and are written exactly anyway: they are what the body says it is doing,
/// and a floor is a fact about today's allocator rather than about this
/// function. It is not the `StringBuilder` wrapper either. It is
/// `Inst::GrowableAlloc`, which ADR 0052 makes allocate **two** objects
/// — the stable owner and the store beneath it — because a builder whose
/// identity moved when it grew would be no use to the recursion `std.vector`
/// and the formatter both rely on. `core.bytesAllocate` called directly here,
/// without a builder, would allocate exactly the same pair. So the second
/// object is the price of building a string in Cove at all, and every string
/// an interpolation builds already pays it; what the intrinsic had instead was
/// a Rust stack to assemble four bytes on before handing the machine a
/// finished run, which is not something a Cove body can be given without a new
/// instruction, and ADR 0064's Decision 2 is the reason not to add one for an
/// operation that `covefmt` and `cq` call **nought** times between them.
///
/// The owner is garbage the instant `finish` runs, which is the one thing that
/// keeps the cost bounded: the bench's heap is one collection against the
/// intrinsic's none, and no live object outlives the call on either side.
///
/// # What the call costs, and what it costs it on
///
/// `benches/frompoint`, medians of three, per call, VM then native:
///
/// ```text
///                 intrinsic          this
///   1 byte        185 / 147     338 / 220
///   2 bytes       185 / 148     488 / 297
///   3 bytes       183 / 148     570 / 322
///   4 bytes       183 / 149     661 / 336
///   surrogate     290 / 261     943 / 708
///   past the end  261 / 233    1181 / 754
/// ```
///
/// Slower on every row, by 1.5x to 4.5x, and flat became sloped: the Rust arm
/// cost the same whatever it encoded and this costs a byte at a time. Two
/// terms are most of it, and a probe over `StringBuilder` alone prices them —
/// the pair of allocations is **142 ns on the VM and 108 native**, and
/// `finish`'s validation and relabel is **59 / 65** for one ASCII byte and
/// **108 / 123** for four non-ASCII ones. On the native tier those two are 69%
/// of the four-byte row; the encode this file writes is the rest. The two
/// refusals are slowest of all because an `Err`'s message is an interpolation
/// here where it was a `format!` there.
///
/// It is a constant factor on an operation the census measured at **0 static
/// sites and 0 dynamic calls on both representative programs**, and it is
/// bounded above by four bytes, so it is not the change of performance class
/// PHILOSOPHY refuses. It is the cost of the arm not being there.
///
/// # The two refusals, and why their order cannot be observed
///
/// A number names no character in exactly two ways, and [`refuseCodePoint`]
/// decides which sentence each gets. The two sets are disjoint — the surrogate
/// hole is strictly inside `0 ..= 0x10FFFF` — so no input is both and the order
/// the three guards below ask their questions in changes no answer. That is
/// why they are written in the order a reader would check them rather than in
/// the order the Rust arm did, which asked about surrogates first.
///
/// # The remainder is a subtraction, because a division is not free
///
/// Each byte past the first needs the low six bits of what is left, and
/// `point % 64` would be a second division of the same pair. `point - low * 64`
/// is a multiply and a subtract instead, so an encoding of `n` bytes costs
/// `n - 1` divisions rather than `2 * (n - 1)`. Measured on `benches/frompoint`
/// at the four-byte row, where the difference is three divisions out of six:
/// **661 ns against 683 on the VM and 336 against 350 native**, which is 3.3%
/// and 4.2%, consistent over three runs of each on both tiers. It is a small
/// number, and it is small because the allocation and the validation above are
/// most of the call — but it is the only arithmetic choice in this body, so it
/// is made the cheap way and written down rather than left to be rediscovered
/// by someone who reads `/` and `%` in [`codePointAtByte`]'s mirror and
/// assumes the symmetry was free.
export fn fromCodePoint(point: Int) -> Result<String, Error> {
  if point < 0 {
    return refuseCodePoint(point)
  }
  if point > 1114111 {
    return refuseCodePoint(point)
  }
  if point >= 55296 {
    if point <= 57343 {
      return refuseCodePoint(point)
    }
  }
  if point < 128 {
    var one = StringBuilder.withCapacity(1)
    one.appendByte(point)
    return Ok(one.finish())
  }
  if point < 2048 {
    var two = StringBuilder.withCapacity(2)
    let lead = point / 64
    two.appendByte(192 + lead)
    two.appendByte(128 + point - lead * 64)
    return Ok(two.finish())
  }
  if point < 65536 {
    var three = StringBuilder.withCapacity(3)
    let low = point / 64
    let lead = low / 64
    three.appendByte(224 + lead)
    three.appendByte(128 + low - lead * 64)
    three.appendByte(128 + point - low * 64)
    return Ok(three.finish())
  }
  var four = StringBuilder.withCapacity(4)
  let low = point / 64
  let middle = low / 64
  let lead = middle / 64
  four.appendByte(240 + lead)
  four.appendByte(128 + middle - lead * 64)
  four.appendByte(128 + low - middle * 64)
  four.appendByte(128 + point - low * 64)
  Ok(four.finish())
}

/// The `Err` that says why `point` names no character, which
/// [`fromCodePoint`] has already found that it does not.
///
/// The surrogates get a sentence of their own because they are the refusal a
/// caller is most likely to be able to do something about. A format that
/// writes a code point in sixteen bits — JSON's `\u`, and UTF-16 generally —
/// writes anything past `0xFFFF` as a *pair* of them, so a program that
/// arrived here with a `0xD800` holds half of a character rather than a bad
/// one, and what it needs to hear is that the other half is still to come.
/// Combining the pair is arithmetic the program does before it calls
/// [`fromCodePoint`]: there is no half-formed value to hand back, because a
/// Cove `String` is UTF-8 and holds no such thing.
///
/// A function of its own for [`refuseRange`]'s reason: the path that succeeds
/// carries no interpolation, and the whole `Result` rather than its `Error` so
/// that each refusal above is a call answered straight into what that body
/// returns.
fn refuseCodePoint(point: Int) -> Result<String, Error> {
  let message = if point >= 55296 && point <= 57343 {
    "`{point}` is a surrogate half, which is not a character on its own"
  } else {
    "`{point}` is not a Unicode code point"
  }
  return Err(Error(message))
}

/// The characters at positions `from` up to but not including `to`.
///
/// Both bounds are **character** positions — the unit [`length`] counts and
/// [`indexOf`] answers in — and both are clamped into `0..length()`, so a
/// `to` at or below `from` answers `""` and no pair of arguments stops the
/// program. A negative bound, a bound past the end, a reversed pair, two
/// bounds past the end and two below it are each answered.
///
/// **It clamps where [`sliceBytes`] refuses, and that difference is the whole
/// of what separates them.** A byte offset outside the string or inside a
/// character is one this type never handed out, so moving it to the nearest
/// legal one would answer a question nobody asked; a character *position* has
/// no such thing as inside, and `Array.slice` and `Vector.slice` clamp the same
/// two bounds the same way for a sequence. `std.array.slice` is this body with
/// no walk under it — the array's index *is* its offset, where a character's is
/// not.
///
/// The positions are turned into byte offsets by [`boundaryAfter`] and the copy
/// beneath that is `core.stringSlice`, ADR 0058's run slice over packed bytes,
/// which allocates the answer at exactly its length and validates nothing. It
/// needs to validate nothing for [`sliceBytes`]' reason: both offsets are where
/// a walk of the lead bytes stopped, so each begins a character or is the end
/// of the string, and a run of valid UTF-8 cut at two character boundaries is
/// valid UTF-8.
///
/// **The two calls are one pass, not two.** The second starts at the offset the
/// first stopped on and walks `stop - start` characters further, so the prefix
/// in front of `from` is read once rather than once per bound. Both
/// subtractions are of non-negative numbers in increasing order — `start` is at
/// least 0 and `stop` at least `start` — so neither can overflow the `Int` that
/// `Int` arithmetic would trap on.
///
/// It was `chars().collect()`, two `i64::clamp`s and a re-collection in Rust,
/// one `IntrinsicCall` per call. ADR 0064 moves it here: `slice` is a method's
/// name and would have to be renamed the day the method was, which is Decision
/// 2's test, and the clamp is a range policy — ADR 0058's table gives ranges
/// and branches to Cove and keeps the bounded copy below. The Rust arm
/// collected the whole receiver into a `Vec<char>` to take two of them;
/// this walks the lead bytes as far as `to` and no further, and allocates only
/// the answer.
///
/// **What the change costs is in `benches/slice`, and it is not one number.**
/// The census measured this operation at 0 sites and 0 calls on both
/// representative programs, and both are unchanged to the instruction by the
/// migration, so the bench is the whole of the measurement. Per call, median
/// of three, against the same rows on the build that had the intrinsic:
///
/// ```text
///                        VM  old -> new     native  old -> new
///   16 B, 5 ch at 0      460 ->   362       457 ->  163
///   88 B, 5 ch at 0      746 ->   362       737 ->  170
///   88 B, 5 ch at 83     755 ->  2628       741 ->  619
///  110 B, 5 ch at 0      696 ->   381       693 ->  186
///  110 B, 5 ch at 35     699 ->  1509       693 ->  436
///   88 B, the whole      909 ->  2749       902 ->  746
///   88 B, empty answer   693 ->   261       693 ->  120
/// ```
///
/// **On the native tier every row is faster**, by 1.2x to 5.8x. On the VM the
/// sign is the walk's: a slice whose `to` is near the front is 1.3x to 2.7x
/// faster, because the Rust arm paid for the whole receiver and this pays for
/// the prefix; a slice at the far end, or of the whole string, is 2.2x to 3.5x
/// slower, because a character step is a VM dispatch where it was a turn of a
/// Rust loop. That is ADR 0064's own trade, and the ratio between the tiers is
/// the reason it is taken: the intrinsic cost the same on both (460 against
/// 457) because a call crosses out of compiled code either way, and this does
/// not.
///
/// **It allocates nothing extra**, which is what Decision 8 gates on: the same
/// bench is 700,038 allocations and 2,500,215 allocated words on both builds,
/// to the word. One `String` per call, as before, and no intermediate.
export fn slice(text: String, from: Int, to: Int) -> String {
  let start = if from < 0 {
    0
  } else {
    from
  }
  let stop = if to < start {
    start
  } else {
    to
  }
  let begin = boundaryAfter(text, 0, start)
  let end = boundaryAfter(text, begin, stop - start)
  return core.stringSlice(text, begin, end - begin)
}

/// The byte offset `characters` characters after byte `at`, or the end of the
/// string if there are fewer than that many left.
///
/// [`charactersBefore`] read the other way round: that one is bounded by a byte
/// offset and counts characters, this one is bounded by a count and answers a
/// byte offset. They share the shape of the step and nothing else — the loops
/// stop on different things and answer different variables — and the one part
/// they really do share is the four-way width `if`, which is
/// [`charactersBefore`]'s reasoning and is stated in full there: a `String` is
/// valid UTF-8 in its shortest form, so the lead byte alone says how wide the
/// character it begins is, and a lead byte of `11111xxx` cannot occur, so the
/// last case needs no test of its own.
///
/// **Factoring that `if` into a shared `characterWidth(lead)` was measured and
/// refused**, by the rule the [`startsWith`] migration set: a factoring is
/// taken only if the lowered sequence at the *existing* caller's sites is
/// unchanged. It is not, and what it costs is paid per character rather than
/// per call, because the shared body is inside the loop. The inliner does
/// expand it — no call survives — but the expansion lands the width in a slot
/// of its own and adds it, where the two copies written out lower to
/// `add.int.imm`, and a constant plus an `add.int` is two instructions for the
/// one that immediate was. At `covefmt.column`, [`length`]'s hot caller, the
/// function is 65 instruction sites and 33,885,902 instructions written out
/// and 67 sites and 35,509,514 factored, over the same 154,986 calls: **plus
/// 1,623,612 instructions, 4.8% of that function**. The whole of covefmt is
/// 934,033,557 instructions against 935,657,169 — and the two deltas are
/// *equal to the instruction*, so `column` is the entire cost and every other
/// caller of this walk is cold. Allocations and allocated words are 4,577,750
/// and 57,661,551 on both sides, so nothing about the heap moved.
///
/// [`charactersBefore`] was taken in the [`indexOf`] round at exactly zero by
/// the same rule, and `matchesAt` was refused in the [`startsWith`] round at
/// +2,178. This is the same rule saying no a second time, which is what makes
/// it a rule rather than a taste.
///
/// **A count bigger than the characters left is not an error here**, it is the
/// clamp: [`slice`] hands this `to` unbounded and reads back the end of the
/// string. The loop therefore has two reasons to stop, and they are an `if` and
/// the `while`'s own test rather than one condition joined by `&&`, for the
/// reason [`sliceBytes`] gives — the arms of a joined condition meet at a
/// shared test that nothing can fuse, where a comparison that is all a branch
/// looks at is one instruction.
fn boundaryAfter(text: String, at: Int, characters: Int) -> Int {
  let length = core.byteLength(text)
  var offset = at
  var left = characters
  while offset < length {
    if left < 1 {
      return offset
    }
    let lead = text.byteAt(offset)
    if lead < 128 {
      offset += 1
    } else if lead < 224 {
      offset += 2
    } else if lead < 240 {
      offset += 3
    } else {
      offset += 4
    }
    left -= 1
  }
  return offset
}

/// The text between two byte offsets, or why there is none.
///
/// Both offsets are **byte** offsets — the ones `byteLength`, `byteAt` and
/// `codePointAtByte` deal in — and the range they name has to be in the string,
/// run forwards, and begin and end at the start of a character (the end of the
/// string counts as one). It refuses rather than clamps, which is where it parts
/// from [`slice`]: an offset outside the string or inside a character is one this
/// type never handed out, and moving it to the nearest legal one would answer a
/// question nobody asked.
///
/// The range is decided here, in Cove, and the copy beneath it is a core
/// intrinsic: ADR 0058's run slice over packed bytes, which allocates the answer
/// at exactly its length and validates nothing. It needs to validate nothing
/// because of the checks above it — a run of valid UTF-8 cut at two character
/// boundaries is valid UTF-8 — so the order below is not a courtesy to the
/// copy, it is the copy's precondition.
///
/// The success path asks only whether `0 <= from <= to <= length` and whether
/// the byte at each end is a continuation byte; which of the five things is
/// wrong, and in what words, is [`refuseRange`]'s, so the common path
/// carries no message.
///
/// **Each question is an `if` of its own rather than one condition joined by
/// `&&` or `||`**, and that is for the machine rather than the reader. A
/// comparison that is all an `if` branches on is one instruction (ADR 0054),
/// but the arms of `a || b` meet at a shared test that nothing can fuse, so
/// joining the five range and byte questions cost this body three more
/// dispatches per call — on a method `examples/covefmt` calls 870 thousand
/// times. The answer is a `return` rather than the body's last expression for
/// the same reason: an `Ok` in tail position is built aside and copied into the
/// answer, which was two dispatches more.
export fn sliceBytes(
  text: String,
  from: Int,
  to: Int,
) -> Result<String, Error> {
  let length = core.byteLength(text)
  if from < 0 {
    return refuseRange(text, from, to)
  }
  if from > to {
    return refuseRange(text, from, to)
  }
  if to > length {
    return refuseRange(text, from, to)
  }
  // A continuation byte is `10xxxxxx`, 128 through 191, and an ASCII byte is
  // below both; the end of the string is a boundary and has no byte to look at.
  if from < length {
    let first = text.byteAt(from)
    if first >= 128 {
      if first < 192 {
        return refuseRange(text, from, to)
      }
    }
  }
  if to < length {
    let last = text.byteAt(to)
    if last >= 128 {
      if last < 192 {
        return refuseRange(text, from, to)
      }
    }
  }
  return Ok(core.stringSlice(text, from, to - from))
}

/// The `Err` that says what is wrong with the byte range `from` to `to` of
/// `text`, which [`sliceBytes`] has already found to be wrong.
///
/// Five things can be wrong, asked in the order a reader would ask them: is
/// `from` an offset into this string at all, is `to`, do they run forwards, and
/// does each begin a character. A range that is wrong in several ways is told
/// about the first. The sentences are the ones `StringBuilder.appendSlice` stops
/// a run with, word for word — a builder's append holds its range to this rule
/// too.
///
/// A function of its own so that the path that succeeds carries no
/// interpolation, and the whole `Result` rather than its `Error` so that each
/// refusal in [`sliceBytes`] is a call answered straight into what that body
/// returns, with nothing built around it afterwards.
fn refuseRange(text: String, from: Int, to: Int) -> Result<String, Error> {
  let length = core.byteLength(text)
  let message = if from < 0 || from > length {
    "`from` is `{from}`, and a byte offset into this string is 0 to {length}"
  } else if to < 0 || to > length {
    "`to` is `{to}`, and a byte offset into this string is 0 to {length}"
  } else if from > to {
    "`from` is `{from}` and `to` is `{to}`, so this range runs backwards"
  } else if from < length && text.byteAt(from) >= 128 && text.byteAt(from) < 192 {
    "`from` is `{from}`, which is inside a character rather than at the start of one"
  } else {
    "`to` is `{to}`, which is inside a character rather than at the start of one"
  }
  return Err(Error(message))
}

/// `parts` with this string between each pair of them.
///
/// **The receiver is the separator**, so it is `", ".join(names)` and never
/// `names.join(", ")`. The schema has declared it that way for as long as the
/// method has existed — `fixed(&[C::Str, C::Strings], C::Str)` — and the
/// parameter order here is that declaration read out loud. It is the one thing
/// about this operation a reader gets wrong, and gets wrong twice.
///
/// # One separator fewer than there are parts
///
/// That sentence is the whole of the arithmetic, and it is why this is Cove
/// rather than an operation of the machine. ADR 0064's Decision 2 asks whether
/// an operation's only failures are ones the program wrote; a count of the
/// *gaps* between things is not a failure at all, it is a policy over a
/// representation, and ADR 0058's table gives those to the standard library.
/// Nothing below it should have to know that joining three things uses two
/// separators.
///
/// It settles three answers at once, and each is a row of
/// `tests/e2e/values_string_join`:
///
/// - an **empty array** is `""`, whatever the separator is — there is no part
///   to write and no gap between two parts to put a separator in, so a
///   three-byte separator contributes nothing. It is the early `return` below,
///   which also makes `count - 1` safe for everything after it;
/// - **one part** is that part, and the separator **is not used**, however
///   wide it is;
/// - `n` **empty** parts under a one-byte separator is `n - 1` bytes, which is
///   the row that tells this arithmetic from an implementation that writes one
///   separator per part.
///
/// # The length is known before a byte is written, and that is what makes the
/// # move affordable
///
/// A join is the one string-building operation that can size its own answer:
/// the parts' lengths summed, plus the separator's times one fewer than the
/// parts. So the loop below sums first and allocates once at exactly that
/// size, which is what `vm::intrinsics::text::joined_bytes` did in Rust with
/// `new_string_of`, and the run **grows nought times**. Measured rather than
/// assumed: `examples/covefmt` reallocates 607,602 stores either side of this
/// change, to the store, and the 31 joins in the e2e corpus — which include a
/// thousand-part one — add none.
///
/// That matters more here than anywhere else in this file, because this is the
/// first operation issue #454 has moved that a shipped program runs in
/// quantity. A body that let the buffer grow instead would pay a reallocation
/// and a copy of everything written so far, per growth, 8,742 times over.
///
/// # Why ADR 0062's window is written out here rather than taken from
/// # `StringBuilder`
///
/// [`fromCodePoint`] above builds its answer through `std.stringbuilder`, and
/// the obvious thing is for this to do the same. It was written that way
/// first, and the two were measured against each other on covefmt over seven
/// interleaved rounds: the builder costs **four more instructions per part** —
/// an `addr-of-slot`, a `load` and two `clear`s per iteration, which is a
/// `var` builder being re-derived from its frame slot every turn of a loop
/// that runs 1,465,687 times — for **+5,910,658 instructions**, 0.59% of the
/// whole program, and 6.015 ms against 5.983 ms at the median, the branch
/// losing 6 pairs of 7. The allocations and the growths are identical either
/// way, so this is a pure instruction-count difference and the sign of it is
/// not in doubt.
///
/// So the three `core.bytes*` calls are here, in the frame of the loop that
/// makes them, which is the same reason `std.stringbuilder.appendText` and
/// `std.int.renderInto` take a `ByteBuffer` rather than a builder: an assembly
/// holds the run, not a handle to it. `let buffer` rather than `var` is the
/// whole of the difference — a growable is ADR 0052's stable owner, so a local
/// binding of it names the same owner an append replaces the store under, and
/// nothing here has to be told when that happens.
///
/// # Two loops, because the separator a real program passes is `""`
///
/// The empty separator gets a loop of its own instead of a `width == 0` test
/// inside one loop, and that is not a guess about which is common. Of
/// `examples/covefmt`'s 8,742 calls, 757 hand over an empty array and return
/// above; **all 7,985 of the rest take the `width == 0` arm**, and the
/// instruction profile of the other arm reports nought executions at every one
/// of its program counters. `"".join(out.freeze())` is how that program
/// assembles every line it prints. So the branch that is never taken is the
/// one with the separator in it, and a comparison per part to find that out
/// would be paid 1,465,687 times.
///
/// # What the move cost
///
/// Counted at `1796f23` against this, both binaries pointed at one pristine
/// root, on `examples/covefmt` — 8,742 calls, 7,985 of them building, over
/// **1,465,687 parts**, which is 184 parts a call:
///
/// ```text
///                        intrinsic          Cove        delta
///   instructions       965,939,026   995,431,204       +3.05%
///   dispatches         915,665,847   936,365,051       +2.26%
///   allocations          4,732,157     4,739,385       +0.15%
///   allocated words     59,723,366    59,751,331      +0.047%
///   collections                 14            14            0
///   growths                607,602       607,602            0
/// ```
///
/// **The cost is instructions and not allocation**, which is the opposite of
/// what the round before this one found for [`fromCodePoint`] and is the whole
/// of why this migration was affordable. Twenty instructions a part — seven in
/// the sum loop and thirteen in the append loop — and against them, per
/// *building* call, one extra allocation and about three and a half extra
/// words: `Inst::GrowableAlloc` makes ADR 0052's owner as well as its store,
/// where `new_string_of` made one object. Spread over a program, that is
/// 0.15% more allocations and 0.047% more allocated words. `examples/cq` calls
/// it four times and moves by 636 instructions, 4 allocations and 12 words.
///
/// **Sizing the run is what keeps the allocation figure that small, and the
/// alternative was measured rather than reasoned about.** The same body with
/// the sum loop deleted and `core.bytesAllocate(0)` in its place answers every
/// row of `tests/e2e/values_string_join` identically and costs 10,323,689
/// fewer instructions — but covefmt's growths rise 607,602 to **626,815**, its
/// allocations by a further 19,213, its **allocated words by 2,522,104
/// (+4.22%)**, and its collections from 14 to 15. So the sum loop buys a
/// hundredfold reduction in the allocation delta for one percent of the
/// program's instructions, and ADR 0064's Decision 8 gates on the allocation.
///
/// **What none of this can tell you is the wall clock.** Both figures above
/// are exact counts. The interleaved timings are not attributable: a control
/// that never calls `join` and executes an instruction stream identical to the
/// instruction — `benches/bytescan` — moves by the same +4.6% the subject
/// does, and four rebuilds of each source, three of them with a code-layout
/// probe inside `vm/exec.rs`, put that difference in two clusters 4.6% apart
/// rather than in one spread. The commit that made this change records the
/// tables and the experiment.
export fn join(separator: String, parts: Array<String>) -> String {
  let count = core.arrayLength(parts)
  if count == 0 {
    return ""
  }
  let width = core.byteLength(separator)
  var total = width * (count - 1)
  for part in parts {
    total += core.byteLength(part)
  }
  let buffer = core.bytesAllocate(total)
  if width == 0 {
    for part in parts {
      let size = core.byteLength(part)
      let at = core.bytesLength(buffer)
      core.bytesEnsure(buffer, size)
      core.bytesCopy(buffer, at, part, 0, size)
      core.bytesCommit(buffer, size)
    }
  } else {
    var first = true
    for part in parts {
      if first {
        first = false
      } else {
        let gap = core.bytesLength(buffer)
        core.bytesEnsure(buffer, width)
        core.bytesCopy(buffer, gap, separator, 0, width)
        core.bytesCommit(buffer, width)
      }
      let size = core.byteLength(part)
      let at = core.bytesLength(buffer)
      core.bytesEnsure(buffer, size)
      core.bytesCopy(buffer, at, part, 0, size)
      core.bytesCommit(buffer, size)
    }
  }
  core.bytesFinish(buffer)
}

/// This string cut at every occurrence of `separator`, which no part holds.
///
/// Every non-overlapping occurrence separates, scanning left to right, so two
/// separators side by side make an empty part between them, a separator at
/// either end makes an empty part there, and text that holds none is one part
/// that is the whole text. `tests/e2e/values_string_split` pins all of it, and
/// was written before this moved.
///
/// # An empty separator stops the run
///
/// It would match between every character, which answers a question other than
/// the one this method asks; [`chars`] is the one that asks it. That is the call
/// that is wrong rather than the text, so it is not an answer: the run stops
/// through `core.refuse` (ADR 0067) with the three sentences [`splitRefused`],
/// [`emptyNeedleRule`] and [`splitHelp`] word, on one line, before `text` is
/// read.
///
/// # One search a part, and no counting pass
///
/// Each separator is found by `core.stringFind`, which is ADR 0065's
/// `Inst::RunFind` — the one bounded byte search below the boundary, charged
/// and polled as a run copy is — and each part is `core.stringSlice`, sliced
/// into a local before the three instructions that publish it, because the
/// window between an ensure and a commit admits nothing that allocates.
///
/// **It is not [`words`]' shape, and that was measured rather than chosen.**
/// [`words`] counts first so its `Vector` is allocated once and never grows,
/// and its count is a byte scan in Cove. Here a count is the same searches
/// again: every separator found twice, which on `benches`' `split_rows` cost
/// more than the growths it saved — `split.wide`, a hundred parts, was 26.9 µs
/// a call counting first and 23.1 µs growing, on both tiers alike, because on
/// the native tier each search is a runtime helper call. So the `Vector` starts
/// with room for four and doubles, a handful of times for a hundred parts.
///
/// It was Rust's `str::split` behind `Intrinsic::StringSplit` until issue
/// #454's Step 3, which could move everything about it but the refusal.
export fn split(text: String, separator: String) -> Array<String> {
  let step = core.byteLength(separator)
  if step == 0 {
    core.refuse(splitRefused(), emptyNeedleRule(), splitHelp())
  }
  let length = core.byteLength(text)
  let parts: Vector<String> = core.vectorWithCapacity(4)
  var start = 0
  var found = core.stringFind(text, separator, 0)
  while found >= 0 {
    let part = core.stringSlice(text, start, found - start)
    let index = core.vectorLength(parts)
    core.vectorEnsure(parts, 1)
    core.vectorStore(parts, index, part)
    core.vectorCommit(parts, 1)
    start = found + step
    found = core.stringFind(text, separator, start)
  }
  let last = core.stringSlice(text, start, length - start)
  let index = core.vectorLength(parts)
  core.vectorEnsure(parts, 1)
  core.vectorStore(parts, index, last)
  core.vectorCommit(parts, 1)
  core.vectorFinish(parts)
}

/// This string with every non-overlapping occurrence of `old` replaced by
/// `new`, scanning left to right.
///
/// `tests/e2e/values_string_replace` pins what that means, and was written
/// before this moved. An empty `old` names nothing to look for, so it stops
/// the run for [`split`]'s reason and in [`split`]'s way, with its own help
/// line: the two methods share the rule and not the suggestion.
///
/// # Text with nothing to replace is answered as it is
///
/// The first search runs before anything is allocated, and if it finds nothing
/// the receiver is the answer. A `String` is immutable, so a program cannot
/// tell it from a copy — the argument issue #104 made for `chars()`'s shared
/// strings — and the Rust arm this replaced built one anyway.
///
/// # One search a match, and room for the receiver
///
/// Not [`join`]'s rule, for [`split`]'s reason: [`join`] can size its answer
/// from lengths it already has, and here the size is a count of matches, which
/// is the searches again. So the buffer starts at the receiver's own length —
/// exact whenever `new` is as long as `old`, and the most common shape
/// otherwise — and grows by doubling when a longer `new` outruns it. Each run
/// between matches and each copy of `new` is an append window of its own:
/// ensure, copy, commit. The two cannot be one window, because a window writes
/// exactly the count it reserved at the length it read, and the verifier holds
/// it to that.
export fn replace(text: String, old: String, new: String) -> String {
  let step = core.byteLength(old)
  if step == 0 {
    core.refuse(replaceRefused(), emptyNeedleRule(), replaceHelp())
  }
  var found = core.stringFind(text, old, 0)
  if found < 0 {
    return text
  }
  let length = core.byteLength(text)
  let width = core.byteLength(new)
  let buffer = core.bytesAllocate(length)
  var start = 0
  while found >= 0 {
    let run = found - start
    let at = core.bytesLength(buffer)
    core.bytesEnsure(buffer, run)
    core.bytesCopy(buffer, at, text, start, run)
    core.bytesCommit(buffer, run)
    let gap = core.bytesLength(buffer)
    core.bytesEnsure(buffer, width)
    core.bytesCopy(buffer, gap, new, 0, width)
    core.bytesCommit(buffer, width)
    start = found + step
    found = core.stringFind(text, old, start)
  }
  let rest = length - start
  let tail = core.bytesLength(buffer)
  core.bytesEnsure(buffer, rest)
  core.bytesCopy(buffer, tail, text, start, rest)
  core.bytesCommit(buffer, rest)
  core.bytesFinish(buffer)
}

/// What [`split`] says of an empty separator.
///
/// This and the four functions after it are the sentences
/// `vm::intrinsics::operand`'s `empty_needle` wrote for both methods, and
/// `builtins`' copy of them for the oracle; they are here and nowhere else now.
/// Functions of their own so that each refusal is one line of its method and
/// the path that answers carries none of them.
fn splitRefused() -> String {
  "`String.split` cannot use an empty `separator`"
}

/// What [`replace`] says of an empty `old`.
fn replaceRefused() -> String {
  "`String.replace` cannot use an empty `old`"
}

/// The rule both refusals are made under.
fn emptyNeedleRule() -> String {
  "An empty separator or search string would match between every character, rather than answer the question the method asks."
}

/// What [`split`] suggests instead.
fn splitHelp() -> String {
  "use `chars()` to take a string apart character by character"
}

/// What [`replace`] suggests instead — not [`splitHelp`], because there is no
/// method that does what an empty `old` would.
fn replaceHelp() -> String {
  "`old` is the text to look for, and an empty `old` names none"
}

/// This string's characters, one one-character `String` each.
///
/// A character in Cove is a `String` of `length()` 1 — there is no `Character`
/// type for this to answer instead — so the answer is an `Array<String>`
/// whose length is the receiver's `length()`, and whose elements are 1, 2, 3
/// or 4 bytes each. The empty string answers an **empty** array and not a
/// one-element one, and a NUL is a character like any other: one element, one
/// byte.
///
/// **It splits by code point and not by grapheme cluster**, which neither the
/// name nor the signature settles, so `tests/e2e/values_string_chars` pins it:
/// a combining acute is an element of its own, a ZWJ family of three people
/// and two joiners is five, a skin-tone modifier is an element, a
/// regional-indicator flag is two, and `CRLF` is two.
///
/// It was `text.chars().map(String::from).collect()` in Rust, one
/// `IntrinsicCall` per call. ADR 0064 moves it here, and what left with it is
/// a **decode**: that arm ran Rust's UTF-8 decoder over the receiver to
/// produce a `char` per character and then ran Rust's encoder over every
/// `char` to put the same bytes back into a fresh heap string. Nothing about
/// the answer needed a scalar — the bytes were already in the receiver in the
/// right order, and a lead byte says how many of them a character is. So this
/// is `core.stringSlice` per character over the walk [`length`] already does,
/// and no code point is ever formed.
///
/// # The two walks, and why the first one is not free
///
/// The run is sized before it is filled: [`charactersBefore`], the private
/// walk under [`length`], counts the characters, and
/// `core.vectorWithCapacity` allocates the store at exactly that many
/// elements. Then the fill walk reads each lead byte again, slices the
/// character out and appends it with ADR 0062's ensure/store/commit.
///
/// **The sizing walk costs about 7.1 instructions a character and the fill
/// loop is about 40 — so this is a 17% tax where `join`'s equivalent was
/// 1%**, and that difference is structural rather than incidental. `join`'s
/// sum loop is proportional to the number of *parts*; this one is
/// proportional to the same thing the fill loop is, because both walk the
/// whole receiver. It is paid anyway, and the reason is the allocation gate.
///
/// **Three sizings were measured rather than reasoned about**, against the
/// intrinsic they replace. `benches/chars` is 32,000 calls on a 61-character
/// ASCII line and `benches/chars_rows` is 4,000 calls each on six receivers
/// whose widths run 1 to 4 bytes:
///
/// ```text
///   benches/chars           intrinsic      this  grown  byteLength
///     instructions         48,256,019   96.19 M    82.27 M    82.24 M
///     allocations           2,016,006 2,048,006  2,208,006 2,048,006
///     allocated words       5,984,025 6,080,025  8,224,025 6,080,025
///     growths                       0         0    160,000         0
///
///   benches/chars_rows      intrinsic      this  grown  byteLength
///     instructions            190,849   40.94 M    28.66 M    28.64 M
///     allocations           1,624,062 1,648,062  1,736,062 1,648,062
///     allocated words       4,824,682 4,896,682  6,504,682 7,248,682
///     growths                      13        13     88,013        13
/// ```
///
/// **Growing the run** — `core.vectorWithCapacity(0)` and let the store
/// double — saves 13,920,000 instructions on `benches/chars` (14.5% of it)
/// and costs **160,000 growths, 160,000 allocations and +2,144,000 allocated
/// words, +37.4%**. Refused on the second number.
///
/// **Sizing by `core.byteLength`** is the interesting one, and it is refused
/// on a row that a measurement of ASCII alone would never have shown. A
/// character is at least one byte, so the byte length is an upper bound on the
/// character count and one header load rather than a walk — and on
/// `benches/chars` it is **exactly** the walk's answer, so that arm is
/// identical to this one on every allocation figure and 14.5% cheaper. Then
/// `benches/chars_rows` puts a four-byte character in it: the store is
/// allocated at four times the elements it holds, `core.vectorFinish`
/// relabels the store in place and keeps it, and allocated words go to
/// **7,248,682 — +50.2% against the intrinsic**. ADR 0064's Decision 8 says
/// allocations and allocated words may not regress at all without a reason
/// accepted in the pull request, and "it is free on ASCII" is not one.
///
/// So the exact count is what is here, at **+1.49% allocated words and +1.48%
/// allocations** against the intrinsic on the width matrix, and the same two
/// figures at +1.60% and +1.59% on the ASCII row. Both are the `Vector`'s
/// owner: `Inst::GrowableAlloc` makes ADR 0052's owner as well as its store,
/// where `make::strings` made one object. Growths are **nought** either way,
/// which is what sizing buys.
///
/// # The slice is taken before the reservation opens, and it has to be
///
/// `core.stringSlice` allocates, and the window between `core.vectorEnsure`
/// and `core.vectorCommit` "admits only what neither calls, allocates,
/// branches nor writes the heap" — the verifier says so by name, and it said
/// so to the first draft of this body. So the character is sliced into a local
/// first and the window is the three instructions that publish it. The local
/// is a frame slot, so the collector finds the fresh string across the
/// `vectorEnsure` that may grow the store, which is ADR 0062's protocol
/// working as written rather than an accident.
///
/// # Sharing the width decision was measured and refused, for the third time
///
/// What this shares with [`charactersBefore`] is that walk itself, for the
/// sizing. What it does **not** share is the four-way width `if`, and the rule
/// is [`startsWith`]'s: a factoring is taken only if the lowered sequence at
/// the *existing* caller's sites is unchanged. Extracting a
/// `characterWidth(lead)` that [`charactersBefore`], [`boundaryAfter`] and
/// this body all call costs `examples/covefmt` **+1,702,626 instructions**,
/// 1,019,881,586 against 1,021,584,212, with allocations, allocated words,
/// growths and collections identical — the inliner expands it, but the
/// expansion lands the width in a slot of its own and adds it, where the copy
/// written out lowers to `add.int.imm`. [`boundaryAfter`] refused the same
/// factoring at +1,623,612 on a smaller corpus and `matchesAt` was refused in
/// the [`startsWith`] round at +2,178. This body needs the width as a *value*
/// anyway — `core.stringSlice` takes a count — so the `if` here is an
/// expression rather than four `+=`s, and that is the whole of the difference.
///
/// # No shipped program calls this
///
/// Measured at `b2fe2f9`: `examples/covefmt` has **0 static sites and 0
/// dynamic calls**, and `examples/cq` has 2 static sites and **0 dynamic
/// calls**, both in `cq/json/json.cove` on paths `revenue-summary` does not
/// take. So covefmt is **count-identical** across this migration — 11,219
/// emitted instructions in 111 functions, 1,019,881,586 encoded instructions,
/// 959,307,452 dispatches, 4,855,264 allocations, 61,227,046 allocated words,
/// 621,730 growths and 15 collections on both sides, and #441's sweep is why
/// the body does not even appear in it — and cq moves only statically: 6,429
/// instructions in 75 functions with 21 `IntrinsicCall` sites become 6,475 in
/// 76 with **19**, and every runtime counter is identical to the digit.
///
/// That makes this the first Step 3 migration whose *representative programs
/// are its own control*, and it is also why no whole-program improvement is
/// claimed for it. What this operation costs is in `benches/chars`, which is
/// `examples/cq`'s scanner loop reduced to the mechanism.
///
/// # What it costs on the clock, and the multiple ADR 0064 asks for
///
/// Nine samples a row of `benches/chars_rows`, medians in nanoseconds a call,
/// two binaries interleaved and pointed at one tree:
///
/// ```text
///   row                    VM intrinsic   VM Cove     x    native both     x
///   empty                            97       174  1.79      96 -> 126  1.31
///   short.ascii  (8 x 1 B)        1,013     1,549  1.53     980 -> 1,070 1.09
///   long.ascii   (128 x 1 B)     12,200    21,334  1.75  12,318 -> 15,071 1.22
///   short.mixed  (8 ch, 20 B)     1,111     1,658  1.49   1,117 -> 1,229 1.10
///   long.mixed   (128 ch, 320 B) 12,831    22,839  1.78  12,729 -> 16,687 1.31
///   four         (128 x 4 B)     16,683    26,447  1.59  16,864 -> 19,649 1.17
/// ```
///
/// **The operation's own multiple is 1.49x to 1.79x on the VM arm**, which is
/// inside Decision 8's 2x, and **1.09x to 1.31x compiled**. The native tier
/// runs every one of these rows faster than the VM does — 0.69 to 0.74 of the
/// VM's time — which is the flip nine of the ten migrations before this one
/// showed and [#464](https://github.com/myuon/cove/issues/464) recorded `join`
/// failing.
///
/// **Nothing here validates, and the rows say so from the other side.**
/// `join`'s round found `core.bytesFinish` re-validating the UTF-8 its body had
/// just written, at +560 ns on 334 non-ASCII bytes. This body cannot pay that:
/// `core.stringSlice` "validates nothing" by its own schema — a run of valid
/// UTF-8 cut at two character boundaries is valid UTF-8 — and
/// `core.vectorFinish` is a word-run relabel. The evidence is `long.ascii`
/// against `four`, the same 128 characters at one byte and at four: the
/// intrinsic widens by **+37%** and this body by **+24%**, so the wide row is
/// where the Cove arm does *relatively best*, which is the opposite of what a
/// per-element validation would do.
export fn chars(text: String) -> Array<String> {
  let length = core.byteLength(text)
  let parts: Vector<String> = core.vectorWithCapacity(charactersBefore(
    text,
    length,
  ))
  var at = 0
  while at < length {
    let lead = text.byteAt(at)
    let width = if lead < 128 {
      1
    } else if lead < 224 {
      2
    } else if lead < 240 {
      3
    } else {
      4
    }
    let part = core.stringSlice(text, at, width)
    let index = core.vectorLength(parts)
    core.vectorEnsure(parts, 1)
    core.vectorStore(parts, index, part)
    core.vectorCommit(parts, 1)
    at += width
  }
  core.vectorFinish(parts)
}

/// This string with every leading and trailing whitespace character removed.
///
/// **Whitespace here is Unicode's `White_Space` property, not ASCII's five
/// bytes**, and that is the one thing about this operation that neither its
/// name nor its signature settles. [`words`] below takes the other answer and
/// the two are written beside each other for that reason: twenty of the
/// twenty-five code points this removes are ordinary characters to that one,
/// and `U+000B` LINE TABULATION is in this set, is one ASCII byte, and is
/// still not one of its five. A single shared predicate under both bodies
/// would be wrong for one of them.
///
/// # The version is pinned here, because nothing else pins it
///
/// The set is [`whiteSpaceWidthAt`]'s, written out as bytes with each code
/// point named, and it is **Unicode 17.0.0** — the version `rustc 1.98.1`
/// reports as `char::UNICODE_VERSION`, which is where the answer came from
/// while this was `str::trim` in the runtime. ADR 0064's Decision 5 asks that
/// Cove own its Unicode version rather than inherit whichever one the Rust
/// toolchain was built against, and until this moved there was nothing in the
/// repository that recorded which that was: a toolchain bump that changed the
/// table would have changed what every Cove program meant, silently.
///
/// `crates/cove-runtime/tests/unicode.rs` is the other half of that. It runs a
/// Cove program that asks this body and [`words`] about **every code point
/// from 0 to `0x10FFFF`** — an exhaustive sweep and not a sample — and asserts
/// that the two sets are exactly the ones `char::is_whitespace` and
/// `char::is_ascii_whitespace` answer, printing the toolchain's own
/// `char::UNICODE_VERSION` when they are not. So a future toolchain whose
/// table differs is a *failing test naming the code point* rather than a
/// semantic drift nobody sees. It is the reproducible generator of Decision 5
/// in miniature: this operation's table is twenty-five code points and is
/// written directly, where `toUpper` and `toLower` need the full case
/// mappings and the generated, checked-in asset that decision describes.
///
/// # What it builds
///
/// One `core.stringSlice`, and nothing else. Both ends walk in by whole
/// characters — forwards by the width [`whiteSpaceWidthAt`] reports, and
/// backwards over the continuation bytes to the lead byte that begins the last
/// character — so what is left is a byte range that begins and ends at a
/// character boundary, which is exactly `core.stringSlice`'s precondition and
/// the argument [`sliceBytes`] makes in full.
///
/// **A receiver with nothing to trim is handed back, not copied.** That is the
/// `from == 0` and `to == length` test at the bottom, and it is the one thing
/// in this body that the intrinsic could not do: `Machine::new_string` built a
/// fresh object out of `str::trim`'s slice whatever the slice was. A `String`
/// is immutable and interchangeable — issue #104's own argument for sharing
/// one, from the other side — so no Cove program can tell the receiver from a
/// copy of it, and `examples/cq` calls this 100,000 times on lines that have
/// nothing to trim.
export fn trim(text: String) -> String {
  let length = core.byteLength(text)
  var from = 0
  while from < length {
    let width = whiteSpaceWidthAt(text, from)
    if width == 0 {
      break
    }
    from += width
  }
  var to = length
  while to > from {
    // Back over the continuation bytes to the lead byte of the last
    // character. A continuation byte is `10xxxxxx`, 128 through 191; `from`
    // begins a character, so the walk stops at or before it either way.
    var start = to - 1
    while start > from {
      let byte = text.byteAt(start)
      if byte < 128 {
        break
      }
      if byte >= 192 {
        break
      }
      start -= 1
    }
    if whiteSpaceWidthAt(text, start) == 0 {
      break
    }
    to = start
  }
  if from == 0 {
    if to == length {
      return text
    }
  }
  core.stringSlice(text, from, to - from)
}

/// The width in bytes of the `White_Space` character beginning at byte `at` of
/// `text`, or 0 if the character that begins there is not one.
///
/// **Unicode 17.0.0's `White_Space`, written out as UTF-8 byte patterns.**
/// Twenty-five code points, in five groups by lead byte, each named:
///
/// ```text
///   1 byte   09 CHARACTER TABULATION   0A LINE FEED      0B LINE TABULATION
///            0C FORM FEED              0D CARRIAGE RETURN
///            20 SPACE
///   C2 85    U+0085 NEXT LINE
///   C2 A0    U+00A0 NO-BREAK SPACE
///   E1 9A 80 U+1680 OGHAM SPACE MARK
///   E2 80 80 U+2000 EN QUAD           .. E2 80 8A U+200A HAIR SPACE
///   E2 80 A8 U+2028 LINE SEPARATOR
///   E2 80 A9 U+2029 PARAGRAPH SEPARATOR
///   E2 80 AF U+202F NARROW NO-BREAK SPACE
///   E2 81 9F U+205F MEDIUM MATHEMATICAL SPACE
///   E3 80 80 U+3000 IDEOGRAPHIC SPACE
/// ```
///
/// **What is not in it is worth as much as what is.** `E2 80 8B` is `U+200B`
/// ZERO WIDTH SPACE, one byte past the end of the `U+2000` run and not
/// whitespace; `EF BB BF` is `U+FEFF`, which is why no lead byte of 239
/// appears here at all; `E2 81 A0` is `U+2060` WORD JOINER; and `E1 A0 8E` is
/// `U+180E`, which *was* whitespace until Unicode 6.3 and is the code point
/// that dates a table. `tests/e2e/values_string_trim` sweeps six bands that
/// bracket every run above, so each of those four is a golden line.
///
/// # Why bytes rather than a code point
///
/// [`codePointAtByte`] is right there and would decode the character for this
/// to compare against a list. It is not used, because the answer this needs is
/// the **width** as well as the verdict — [`trim`] advances by it — and
/// because a decode reassembles a number out of bytes that a comparison would
/// then take apart again. Every question below is a byte already in the
/// receiver, and the common case, an ASCII character that is not whitespace,
/// is settled by one load and two comparisons.
///
/// The lead bytes are asked in the order a text makes them likely: ASCII
/// first, then `C2` — `U+00A0` is the one non-ASCII whitespace that appears in
/// prose people write — then the `U+2000` block, then the two singletons.
///
/// A `String` is valid UTF-8 in its shortest form, so a lead byte of `C2` is
/// followed by one continuation byte and a lead byte of `E1`, `E2` or `E3` by
/// two. That is why nothing below checks the length before reading `at + 1` or
/// `at + 2`, and it is [`codePointAtByte`]'s reasoning rather than a new one.
fn whiteSpaceWidthAt(text: String, at: Int) -> Int {
  let lead = text.byteAt(at)
  if lead < 128 {
    if lead == 32 {
      return 1
    }
    if lead < 9 {
      return 0
    }
    if lead <= 13 {
      return 1
    }
    return 0
  }
  // C2 85 is U+0085 NEXT LINE and C2 A0 is U+00A0 NO-BREAK SPACE.
  if lead == 194 {
    let second = text.byteAt(at + 1)
    if second == 133 {
      return 2
    }
    if second == 160 {
      return 2
    }
    return 0
  }
  if lead == 226 {
    let second = text.byteAt(at + 1)
    if second == 128 {
      // 80 through 8A is U+2000 EN QUAD through U+200A HAIR SPACE; 8B is
      // U+200B ZERO WIDTH SPACE, which is not whitespace. Then U+2028 LINE
      // SEPARATOR, U+2029 PARAGRAPH SEPARATOR and U+202F NARROW NO-BREAK
      // SPACE.
      let third = text.byteAt(at + 2)
      if third <= 138 {
        return 3
      }
      if third == 168 {
        return 3
      }
      if third == 169 {
        return 3
      }
      if third == 175 {
        return 3
      }
      return 0
    }
    // E2 81 9F is U+205F MEDIUM MATHEMATICAL SPACE.
    if second == 129 {
      if text.byteAt(at + 2) == 159 {
        return 3
      }
    }
    return 0
  }
  // E1 9A 80 is U+1680 OGHAM SPACE MARK.
  if lead == 225 {
    if text.byteAt(at + 1) == 154 {
      if text.byteAt(at + 2) == 128 {
        return 3
      }
    }
    return 0
  }
  // E3 80 80 is U+3000 IDEOGRAPHIC SPACE, the last of the twenty-five.
  if lead == 227 {
    if text.byteAt(at + 1) == 128 {
      if text.byteAt(at + 2) == 128 {
        return 3
      }
    }
  }
  return 0
}

/// This string's words, split on ASCII whitespace.
///
/// **Five bytes, not twenty-five code points**: `09` tab, `0A` line feed, `0C`
/// form feed, `0D` carriage return and `20` space. [`trim`] above takes the
/// other answer, and `U+000B` LINE TABULATION is the row that separates them —
/// it is `White_Space`, it is one ASCII byte, and it is not one of these five.
/// `tests/e2e/values_string_words` sweeps the same six bands
/// `tests/e2e/values_string_trim` does and answers `parts 1` on twenty of the
/// twenty-five.
///
/// **Nothing here decodes, and nothing here needs to.** Every separator is one
/// ASCII byte, and a byte below 128 can only be a character of its own in
/// UTF-8 — a continuation byte is 128 or above and so is every lead byte of a
/// multi-byte character — so a scan that compares bytes finds exactly the
/// separators a scan that compared characters would find, and every part
/// boundary it cuts at is a character boundary. That is [`startsWith`]'s
/// argument at every offset rather than at one, and it is why this body has no
/// width `if` where [`chars`] and [`trim`] both do.
///
/// Runs of separators collapse and the ends are dropped, so no part this
/// answers is ever empty and a receiver that is all separators answers no
/// parts at all.
///
/// # The store is sized exactly, and that costs more here than anywhere else
///
/// [`wordCount`] is a **second scan of the whole receiver**, and it is there
/// because the two arms that avoid it both regress what ADR 0064's Decision 8
/// says may not regress. Measured on `benches/trimwords`' `words_rows`,
/// against the intrinsic this replaced:
///
/// ```text
///   sizing                     allocations   allocated words   instructions
///   the intrinsic                3,200,091        10,553,933        559,237
///   grown from nought            3,448,091        13,993,933    263,919,493
///   an upper bound of len/2      3,272,091        20,873,933    263,991,493
///   exact, this body             3,272,091        10,769,933    492,727,512
/// ```
///
/// **The grown arm is +32.6% allocated words** — `core.vectorEnsure` doubles,
/// and 2.44 growths a call is 176,000 reallocations the exact arm does not
/// make. **The upper bound is +97.8%**, which is issue #454's Step 3 finding
/// repeated at a different operation: a store allocated at half the receiver's
/// byte length for a receiver that holds one word is relabelled in place by
/// `core.vectorFinish` and kept, so `one.long` pays 512 element slots for one
/// part. Neither number is one Decision 8 admits, and "it is nearly free on a
/// string with many short words" is not a reason.
///
/// The exact arm is **+2.3% allocations and +2.0% allocated words**, and both
/// of those are structural rather than sizing: the 72,000 extra allocations
/// are exactly one per call, `Inst::GrowableAlloc`'s ADR 0052 owner beside its
/// store where `make::strings` allocated one object. Growths are **nought**.
///
/// **The tax is +86.7% instructions, and that is the largest of the series.**
/// [`chars`]' pre-pass was +17% and `join`'s 1%, and the difference is not the
/// size of this body: a character count is a lead-byte *walk*, which is
/// cheaper than the per-character slice it sizes, where a word count is the
/// **same scan** the fill loop is, byte for byte. So sizing this run exactly
/// is the scan done twice, and there is no cheaper question to ask.
///
/// It is affordable because **no shipped program calls this at all** — nought
/// static sites and nought dynamic calls in both `examples/covefmt` and
/// `examples/cq` — so what the tax is paid on is `benches/trimwords` and the
/// two `.words()` calls in `examples/`, which run once each.
export fn words(text: String) -> Array<String> {
  let length = core.byteLength(text)
  let parts: Vector<String> = core.vectorWithCapacity(wordCount(text, length))
  var at = 0
  while at < length {
    while at < length {
      let byte = text.byteAt(at)
      if byte > 32 {
        break
      }
      if !separates(byte) {
        break
      }
      at += 1
    }
    if at == length {
      break
    }
    let start = at
    while at < length {
      let byte = text.byteAt(at)
      if byte <= 32 {
        if separates(byte) {
          break
        }
      }
      at += 1
    }
    // `core.stringSlice` allocates, and the window between `core.vectorEnsure`
    // and `core.vectorCommit` admits nothing that does — the verifier says so
    // by name. So the part is sliced into a local first and the window is the
    // three instructions that publish it, which is [`chars`]' shape and its
    // reason.
    let part = core.stringSlice(text, start, at - start)
    let index = core.vectorLength(parts)
    core.vectorEnsure(parts, 1)
    core.vectorStore(parts, index, part)
    core.vectorCommit(parts, 1)
  }
  core.vectorFinish(parts)
}

/// How many words `text` holds in its first `length` bytes.
///
/// [`words`]' own scan with the slicing taken out, so that the `Vector` below
/// it is allocated once at exactly its length and grows **nought** times. It
/// is the most expensive pre-pass of the ADR 0064 series and the pull request
/// that moved this operation has the three arms it was chosen over; the short
/// of it is that a word count is the *same scan* the fill loop is, where
/// [`chars`]' count is a lead-byte walk that is cheaper than its own fill.
///
/// The two inner loops are [`words`]' two, byte for byte, and the reason they
/// are copied rather than shared is [`startsWith`]' rule: a factoring is taken
/// only if the lowered sequence at the existing caller's sites is unchanged,
/// and a shared "skip to the next separator" body would land its answer in a
/// slot and return it where the loop written out lowers to a branch.
fn wordCount(text: String, length: Int) -> Int {
  var count = 0
  var at = 0
  while at < length {
    while at < length {
      let byte = text.byteAt(at)
      if byte > 32 {
        break
      }
      if !separates(byte) {
        break
      }
      at += 1
    }
    if at == length {
      break
    }
    count += 1
    while at < length {
      let byte = text.byteAt(at)
      if byte <= 32 {
        if separates(byte) {
          break
        }
      }
      at += 1
    }
  }
  count
}

/// Whether `byte` is one of the five ASCII whitespace bytes [`words`] splits
/// on.
///
/// `09` CHARACTER TABULATION, `0A` LINE FEED, `0C` FORM FEED, `0D` CARRIAGE
/// RETURN and `20` SPACE. **`0B` LINE TABULATION is not one of them**, and the
/// `byte == 11` line below is the only line in this file that exists to say
/// no: it is the difference between this set and [`trim`]'s, and a reader who
/// assumed "the ASCII control characters and a space" would write it without.
///
/// The questions are in the order a text makes them likely rather than in the
/// order a reader would write them. Every byte of a word is above 32 — letters,
/// digits, punctuation, and every byte of every multi-byte character — so
/// `byte > 32` is asked first and settles the common case in one comparison,
/// where a set written as `byte == 9 || byte == 10 || ...` would ask five. Each
/// question is an `if` of its own with a `return` for [`sliceBytes`]' reason:
/// a comparison that is all an `if` branches on is one instruction, and the
/// arms of `a || b` meet at a shared test that nothing can fuse.
fn separates(byte: Int) -> Bool {
  if byte > 32 {
    return false
  }
  if byte == 32 {
    return true
  }
  if byte < 9 {
    return false
  }
  if byte > 13 {
    return false
  }
  if byte == 11 {
    return false
  }
  return true
}

/// This string with every character replaced by its uppercase mapping.
///
/// **Unicode's full uppercase mapping, at a version this file names.** It is
/// **Unicode 17.0.0** — what `rustc 1.98.1` reports as `char::UNICODE_VERSION`,
/// and where the answer came from while this was `str::to_uppercase` in the
/// runtime. ADR 0064's Decision 5 asks that Cove own its Unicode version
/// rather than inherit whichever one the Rust toolchain was built against.
/// [`upperRuns`] and [`upperExpansions`] are that version packed into two
/// literals, and `crates/cove-runtime/tests/unicode.rs` sweeps **every code
/// point from 0 to `0x10FFFF`** against the toolchain's own
/// `char::to_uppercase`, so a future toolchain whose table differs is a
/// *failing test naming the code point*.
///
/// # Full, not simple, and so not length-preserving
///
/// A character may uppercase to more than one. `ß` is `SS`, `ﬃ` is `FFI`, `և`
/// is `ԵՒ`, and `ΰ` is three characters. **102 code points expand** — 86 to two
/// characters and 16 to three — and the worst of them trebles the bytes. So
/// the answer's length is computed rather than assumed, and the first of the
/// two walks below is what computes it.
///
/// **No rule here depends on context.** An uppercase answer is a function of
/// one character, and that is asserted exhaustively rather than believed:
/// every code point in four multi-character contexts agrees with the same code
/// point alone. [`toLower`] is the one of the pair with a context rule, and
/// the asymmetry is the most surprising thing about them.
///
/// **There is no locale.** `i` uppercases to `I` and never to `İ`, whatever
/// language the caller is writing, because `SpecialCasing.txt`'s Turkish and
/// Lithuanian rows are conditional on a locale this language does not have.
/// `tests/e2e/values_string_to_upper` pins that as a row rather than leaving it
/// to be assumed of a reader who knows the Turkish rule exists.
///
/// # A receiver with nothing to change is handed back
///
/// The first walk answers *whether* as well as *how long*, and a receiver that
/// uppercases to itself returns without allocating at all — `"HELLO"`, a CJK
/// string, an emoji, a run of digits. That is [`trim`]'s `return text` for
/// [`trim`]'s reason: a `String` is immutable and interchangeable, so no Cove
/// program can tell the receiver from a copy of it, and the intrinsic could not
/// do it because `Machine::new_string` copied whatever `str::to_uppercase` had
/// built.
///
/// # Why two walks, and why the second one is nearly free
///
/// The store is sized **exactly**, which costs a walk of the receiver before
/// the one that fills it. Measured on `benches/casemap`'s `upper_rows`:
///
/// ```text
///   sizing                    allocations   allocated words   instructions
///   grown from nought              50,090           723,357    110,733,701
///   an upper bound of 3 × len      24,090           953,357    110,733,701
///   exact, this body               24,090           357,357    113,513,701
/// ```
///
/// **Grown is +107.7% allocated words and +107.9% allocations**; the upper
/// bound is **+166.8% words**, and it has to be three times the receiver
/// because that is what `ΰ` does — so every ASCII string in the world would
/// take three bytes a character to hold one, and an oversized store is
/// *relabelled* rather than trimmed, so it is permanently allocated. Neither
/// is a number ADR 0064's Decision 8 admits.
///
/// **Exact costs +2.5% instructions, where the same trade cost `chars` 17%
/// and `words` 86.7%.** The reason is the row above the table rather than
/// anything about case mapping: the `return text` branch *already needs a
/// whole walk*, because a receiver maps to itself only if every character
/// does. So all three arms pay for the walk and for the table lookup in it,
/// and what exact adds on top is one `mappedWidth` and one addition a
/// character. Where a sizing pre-pass is usually a second scan bought to
/// avoid reallocation, here it is a column added to a scan that was going to
/// happen anyway.
///
/// The pass is cheap where it matters for a second reason: an ASCII character
/// is settled by one load and two comparisons and never reaches the table.
export fn toUpper(text: String) -> String {
  let runs = upperRuns()
  let length = core.byteLength(text)
  var size = 0
  var changed = false
  var at = 0
  while at < length {
    let lead = text.byteAt(at)
    if lead < 128 {
      // The only ASCII characters with an uppercase mapping are `a` through
      // `z`, and each maps within ASCII — so the common case never reaches
      // the table and never changes a width. `crates/cove-runtime/tests/unicode.rs`
      // is what keeps that true of the table rather than of this comment.
      size += 1
      at += 1
      if lead >= 97 {
        if lead <= 122 {
          changed = true
        }
      }
    } else {
      let width = characterWidth(lead)
      let point = pointAt(text, at, lead)
      let mapping = caseMappingOf(runs, point)
      if mapping == point {
        size += width
      } else {
        changed = true
        size += mappedWidth(mapping)
      }
      at += width
    }
  }
  if changed == false {
    return text
  }
  let expansions = upperExpansions()
  let buffer = core.bytesAllocate(size)
  var from = 0
  while from < length {
    let lead = text.byteAt(from)
    if lead < 128 {
      var byte = lead
      if lead >= 97 {
        if lead <= 122 {
          byte = lead - 32
        }
      }
      let to = core.bytesLength(buffer)
      core.bytesEnsure(buffer, 1)
      core.bytesStore(buffer, to, byte)
      core.bytesCommit(buffer, 1)
      from += 1
    } else {
      let width = characterWidth(lead)
      let point = pointAt(text, from, lead)
      let mapping = caseMappingOf(runs, point)
      if mapping == point {
        let to = core.bytesLength(buffer)
        core.bytesEnsure(buffer, width)
        core.bytesCopy(buffer, to, text, from, width)
        core.bytesCommit(buffer, width)
      } else {
        writeMapping(buffer, mapping, expansions)
      }
      from += width
    }
  }
  core.bytesFinish(buffer)
}

/// This string with every character replaced by its lowercase mapping.
///
/// [`toUpper`]'s mirror in everything but the one thing that matters: **this
/// direction has a rule that depends on a character's neighbours**, and it is
/// the reason a per-character table is not on its own enough.
///
/// # The final sigma
///
/// `Σ` lowercases to `ς` at the end of a word and to `σ` everywhere else.
/// `"ΑΣ"` is `"ας"`; `"ΑΣΑ"` is `"ασα"`; `"Σ"` alone is `"σ"`, because there is
/// no word for it to be the end of; and `"ΣΣ"` is `"σς"`, which is both answers
/// in one string. Unicode calls the condition `Final_Sigma` and it is the only
/// conditional case mapping in `SpecialCasing.txt` that is not conditional on a
/// *locale* — which is why it is here and the Turkish rows are not.
///
/// [`sigmaIsFinal`] is the rule. What "a word" means there is two Unicode
/// properties, `Cased` and `Case_Ignorable`, and they are [`casedRanges`] and
/// [`ignorableRanges`]. The subtlety that decides 268 code points is written
/// down at [`sigmaIsFinal`] rather than here.
///
/// # One character expands, and it is not the one people expect
///
/// `İ` — `U+0130`, the Turkish dotted capital I — lowercases to **`i` followed
/// by a combining dot above**, and it is the *only* code point whose lowercase
/// mapping is more than one character. Uppercasing has 102. So this direction
/// is very nearly length-preserving and still is not, and
/// `tests/e2e/values_string_to_lower` has the row.
///
/// **`ẞ` does not lowercase to `SS`.** `U+1E9E` lowercases to `ß`, one
/// character, and it is `ß` that uppercases to two. The pair is not a
/// bijection, which is why `toUpper(toLower(x))` is not `toUpper(x)` in
/// general — `U+212A` KELVIN SIGN is the shortest demonstration, and the
/// corpus's `round` rows are where both files say so.
export fn toLower(text: String) -> String {
  let runs = lowerRuns()
  let length = core.byteLength(text)
  var size = 0
  var changed = false
  var at = 0
  while at < length {
    let lead = text.byteAt(at)
    if lead < 128 {
      // `A` through `Z`, within ASCII, and nothing else below 128.
      size += 1
      at += 1
      if lead >= 65 {
        if lead <= 90 {
          changed = true
        }
      }
    } else {
      let width = characterWidth(lead)
      let point = pointAt(text, at, lead)
      let mapping = caseMappingOf(runs, point)
      if mapping == point {
        size += width
      } else {
        changed = true
        size += mappedWidth(mapping)
      }
      at += width
    }
  }
  if changed == false {
    return text
  }
  let expansions = lowerExpansions()
  let buffer = core.bytesAllocate(size)
  var from = 0
  while from < length {
    let lead = text.byteAt(from)
    if lead < 128 {
      var byte = lead
      if lead >= 65 {
        if lead <= 90 {
          byte = lead + 32
        }
      }
      let to = core.bytesLength(buffer)
      core.bytesEnsure(buffer, 1)
      core.bytesStore(buffer, to, byte)
      core.bytesCommit(buffer, 1)
      from += 1
    } else {
      let width = characterWidth(lead)
      let point = pointAt(text, from, lead)
      // `U+03A3` is the one character whose answer the table cannot give,
      // and `U+03C3` and `U+03C2` are the two it chooses between. Both are
      // two bytes, which is why the sizing walk above needs none of this:
      // the final sigma changes what is written and never how much.
      if point == 931 {
        var answer = 963
        if sigmaIsFinal(text, from, length) {
          answer = 962
        }
        writePoint(buffer, answer)
      } else {
        let mapping = caseMappingOf(runs, point)
        if mapping == point {
          let to = core.bytesLength(buffer)
          core.bytesEnsure(buffer, width)
          core.bytesCopy(buffer, to, text, from, width)
          core.bytesCommit(buffer, width)
        } else {
          writeMapping(buffer, mapping, expansions)
        }
      }
      from += width
    }
  }
  core.bytesFinish(buffer)
}

/// Whether the `Σ` beginning at byte `at` of `text` ends a word.
///
/// Unicode's `Final_Sigma`: the sigma is preceded by a `Cased` character and
/// not followed by one, in both cases **skipping `Case_Ignorable` characters**
/// on the way. So `"ΑΣ'"` is a final sigma — the apostrophe is ignorable and
/// the alpha is cased — and `"ΑΣa"` is not.
///
/// # The rule that decides 268 code points
///
/// `Cased` and `Case_Ignorable` **overlap**. A modifier letter such as `ʰ` is
/// both: it is `Other_Lowercase`, so it is cased, and it is `Lm`, so it is
/// ignorable. 268 code points are in both sets, and what happens at `"ΑʰΣ"`
/// depends entirely on which answer is taken first.
///
/// **The skip comes first, so a character that is both is skipped.** `"ΑʰΣ"`
/// lowercases to `"αʰς"`: the `ʰ` is stepped over and the `Α` behind it is what
/// satisfies the condition. An implementation that asked "cased?" first would
/// answer `"αʰσ"` — it would stop at the `ʰ`, find it cased, and be right for
/// the wrong reason on this row while being wrong on `"ΑʰΣa"`.
///
/// This is not a reading of the Rust source. It was established against the
/// binary that still had the intrinsic, by asking `str::to_lowercase` about
/// every code point in that position, and then reconciled against Unicode
/// 17.0.0's published `DerivedCoreProperties.txt`: the two agree exactly once
/// the both-sets rule above is applied, and differ by exactly those 268
/// without it. [`casedRanges`] therefore holds `Cased` **minus**
/// `Case_Ignorable`, and the two tables below are disjoint by construction.
///
/// # What it costs
///
/// A scan in each direction, per sigma, so a string of `n` sigmas costs
/// `O(n²)` in the worst case. That is the shape `str::to_lowercase` had too —
/// it scanned the same two directions from each sigma — so it is not a change
/// of class, and no other character in the receiver pays anything at all,
/// because this is reached only from the `U+03A3` arm.
fn sigmaIsFinal(text: String, at: Int, length: Int) -> Bool {
  let cased = casedRanges()
  let ignorable = ignorableRanges()
  var before = false
  var back = at
  while back > 0 {
    // Back over the continuation bytes to the lead byte of the previous
    // character: a continuation byte is `10xxxxxx`, 128 through 191.
    // [`trim`] walks backwards the same way and for the same reason.
    var start = back - 1
    while start > 0 {
      let byte = text.byteAt(start)
      if byte < 128 {
        break
      }
      if byte >= 192 {
        break
      }
      start -= 1
    }
    let point = pointAt(text, start, text.byteAt(start))
    if inPackedRanges(ignorable, point) == false {
      before = inPackedRanges(cased, point)
      break
    }
    back = start
  }
  if before == false {
    return false
  }
  // Forwards from the character after the sigma, which is two bytes wide.
  var ahead = at + 2
  while ahead < length {
    let lead = text.byteAt(ahead)
    let point = pointAt(text, ahead, lead)
    if inPackedRanges(ignorable, point) == false {
      return inPackedRanges(cased, point) == false
    }
    ahead += characterWidth(lead)
  }
  true
}

/// What `point` maps to under `runs`, or `point` itself when it maps to
/// nothing.
///
/// The answer is one `Int` and carries two cases, because a Cove function
/// answers one value and a pair would be an allocation on a path that runs
/// once a character. A **non-negative** answer is the code point to write. A
/// **negative** answer is `0 - 1 - packed`, where `packed` names a slice of the
/// expansion text — [`writeMapping`] and [`mappedWidth`] are the two readers
/// and neither needs to know which case it has until it asks.
///
/// # The table
///
/// A record is thirteen base-32 symbols and the records are sorted by their
/// first field, so this is a binary search: nine probes for the uppercase
/// table's 302 records, and no allocation, no growth, and no walk. The fields
/// are the start code point (five symbols), how many code points the run
/// covers (two), the stride (one), the kind (one) and the payload (four).
///
/// **The stride is one or two**, because case mappings come in both shapes:
/// `U+0041` through `U+005A` is a run of consecutive code points with the same
/// delta, and `U+0100`, `U+0102`, `U+0104` … is every *other* code point, the
/// Latin Extended-A pattern where an uppercase letter and its lowercase are
/// adjacent. Allowing the second shape takes the uppercase table from 688
/// records to 302. The runs never overlap — the generator asserts it — so
/// "the last record starting at or below `point`" is the only record that can
/// hold it.
fn caseMappingOf(runs: String, point: Int) -> Int {
  let record = packedRecordFor(runs, 13, point)
  if record < 0 {
    return point
  }
  let at = record * 13
  let offset = point - packedStart(runs, at)
  let stride = runs.byteAt(at + 7) - 48
  // The run's last member, as an offset from its first. It is a local rather
  // than written into the comparison because `examples/covefmt` does not
  // reproduce a parenthesised expression on the right of a bare `>` — see
  // issue #469 — and `cove fmt --check` cannot see that, only
  // `covefmtBench` can.
  let reach = (packedField(runs, at + 5, 2) - 1) * stride
  if offset > reach {
    return point
  }
  if stride == 2 {
    if offset - offset / 2 * 2 != 0 {
      return point
    }
  }
  let payload = packedField(runs, at + 9, 4)
  // Kind `0` is a delta, biased so that it is never negative in the table;
  // kind `1` is a slice of the expansion text.
  if runs.byteAt(at + 8) == 48 {
    return point + payload - 524288
  }
  0 - 1 - payload
}

/// Whether `point` is inside one of `table`'s ranges.
///
/// [`packedRecordFor`]'s other reader, over the two-field records
/// [`casedRanges`] and [`ignorableRanges`] are made of: a start code point in
/// five symbols and a count in two.
fn inPackedRanges(table: String, point: Int) -> Bool {
  let record = packedRecordFor(table, 7, point)
  if record < 0 {
    return false
  }
  let at = record * 7
  point < packedStart(table, at) + packedField(table, at + 5, 2)
}

/// The index of the last record of `table` whose first field is at most
/// `point`, or `0 - 1` when every record starts above it.
///
/// The bounded lookup ADR 0064's Decision 5 asks for, and the only kind of
/// lookup a packed literal admits. `table` is a `String` literal, so
/// [ADR 0045](../../../docs/adr/0045-a-literal-is-there-before-the-program-runs.md)
/// has already placed it in the heap before the first instruction of the run:
/// the call that named it is a load of an address, and every `byteAt` below is
/// one `Inst::RunLoad` against an object that is never collected, never copied
/// and never rebuilt. There is nothing here that allocates, so nothing here is
/// refused inside a reservation either.
fn packedRecordFor(table: String, width: Int, point: Int) -> Int {
  var low = 0
  var high = core.byteLength(table) / width
  // Every record below `low` starts at or below `point`, and every record at
  // or above `high` starts above it.
  while low < high {
    let middle = low + (high - low) / 2
    if packedStart(table, middle * width) <= point {
      low = middle + 1
    } else {
      high = middle
    }
  }
  low - 1
}

/// The five-symbol code point beginning at byte `at` of `table`: the first
/// field of every record, and the only one a search reads.
///
/// [`packedField`]'s loop written out, which is worth a helper because it is
/// the one field read more than once a character: a binary search over
/// `upperRuns`' 302 records asks it nine times, and `toUpper` searches twice —
/// once to size the answer and once to build it. Unrolling the five turns is
/// worth **1.25x to 1.36x** on every row that reads the table —
/// `benches/casemap`'s `latin.one` 5,929 ns a call to 4,737, `greek.run`
/// 41,422 to 30,473, `han.run` 41,538 to 31,117 — and it moves the ASCII rows,
/// which never reach a table at all, by less than the spread between two runs.
fn packedStart(table: String, at: Int) -> Int {
  var value = table.byteAt(at) - 48
  value = value * 32 + table.byteAt(at + 1) - 48
  value = value * 32 + table.byteAt(at + 2) - 48
  value = value * 32 + table.byteAt(at + 3) - 48
  value * 32 + table.byteAt(at + 4) - 48
}

/// The `width`-symbol base-32 field beginning at byte `at` of `table`.
///
/// **The alphabet is `0` through `O` — the 32 bytes from 48 to 79 — and a
/// symbol's value is its byte less 48.** A contiguous run, so the decode is a
/// subtraction rather than a lookup, and one that contains none of `"`, `\`,
/// `{` and `}`: the tables are written into this file as ordinary string
/// literals with no escape in them, which is what makes them diffable and what
/// makes the formatter reproduce them untouched.
///
/// Thirty-two rather than sixty-four because there is no window of 64
/// contiguous printable bytes that misses the backslash. Five bits a symbol
/// costs about a fifth more than six would; the whole asset is eleven
/// kilobytes either way.
fn packedField(table: String, at: Int, width: Int) -> Int {
  var value = 0
  var step = 0
  while step < width {
    value = value * 32 + table.byteAt(at + step) - 48
    step += 1
  }
  value
}

/// How many bytes [`caseMappingOf`]'s answer writes.
fn mappedWidth(mapping: Int) -> Int {
  if mapping < 0 {
    let packed = 0 - 1 - mapping
    return packed - packed / 16 * 16
  }
  if mapping < 128 {
    return 1
  }
  if mapping < 2048 {
    return 2
  }
  if mapping < 65536 {
    return 3
  }
  4
}

/// How wide the character beginning with the lead byte `lead` is.
///
/// [`charactersBefore`]'s four cases, as an answer rather than as a step. A
/// `String` is valid UTF-8 in its shortest form, so the lead byte alone says
/// how wide the character it begins is and a lead byte of `11111xxx` cannot
/// occur — which is why the last case needs no test.
fn characterWidth(lead: Int) -> Int {
  if lead < 128 {
    return 1
  }
  if lead < 224 {
    return 2
  }
  if lead < 240 {
    return 3
  }
  4
}

/// The code point of the character at byte `at` of `text`, whose lead byte the
/// caller has already read as `lead`.
///
/// [`codePointAtByte`] without the `Option`, and that is the whole of the
/// difference. That one is the API and answers `None` for an offset outside
/// the string or inside a character; every caller here has just read `lead`
/// from a boundary it walked to itself, so the question is already settled and
/// an `Option` would be a wrapper allocated and unwrapped once a character.
fn pointAt(text: String, at: Int, lead: Int) -> Int {
  if lead < 128 {
    return lead
  }
  let second = text.byteAt(at + 1) - 128
  if lead < 224 {
    return (lead - 192) * 64 + second
  }
  let third = text.byteAt(at + 2) - 128
  if lead < 240 {
    return ((lead - 224) * 64 + second) * 64 + third
  }
  (((lead - 240) * 64 + second) * 64 + third) * 64 + text.byteAt(at + 3) - 128
}

/// Appends what [`caseMappingOf`] answered to `buffer`.
///
/// An expansion is **one `core.bytesCopy` out of the expansion literal**, not
/// an encode of two or three code points: the literal holds the answers as the
/// UTF-8 they already are, so the payload is an offset and a length and the
/// append is a run copy. 72 distinct expansions share 317 bytes, because an
/// answer that is a suffix of one already written is not written twice.
fn writeMapping(buffer: ByteBuffer, mapping: Int, expansions: String) {
  if mapping < 0 {
    let packed = 0 - 1 - mapping
    let from = packed / 16
    let count = packed - from * 16
    let at = core.bytesLength(buffer)
    // Everything the copy needs is a local *before* the reservation opens.
    // Between an `ensure` and its `commit` the verifier admits only what
    // cannot fail, and arithmetic can overflow — so a `packed / 16` written
    // where it is used is refused, naming the reservation rather than the
    // division.
    core.bytesEnsure(buffer, count)
    core.bytesCopy(buffer, at, expansions, from, count)
    core.bytesCommit(buffer, count)
    return
  }
  writePoint(buffer, mapping)
}

/// Appends `point` to `buffer` as UTF-8.
///
/// [`fromCodePoint`]'s encoder over a buffer that already exists, and without
/// its three refusals: `point` here is a code point a table answered or the
/// small sigma [`toLower`] chose, so there is no number to refuse. The
/// remainder is a subtraction rather than a second division, which is the
/// arithmetic choice [`fromCodePoint`] measured.
///
/// **Each byte is its own ensure, store and commit**, which is not a style.
/// ADR 0062's reservation admits exactly *one* write, at an index known to be
/// the store's own length and for the count the `ensure` named — so a
/// three-byte character cannot be three stores inside one window, and it is
/// not a matter of hoisting the arithmetic out either: `at + 1` is not the
/// length the reservation was opened at. Writing it the other way is refused
/// by `cove_ir::verify`, naming the reservation rather than the store, and
/// that refusal is the thing to remember about appending anything wider than
/// a byte from Cove.
///
/// So this is [`appendUtf8Byte`] one to four times, which is what
/// `std.stringbuilder.appendByteInto` is and what
/// [`fromCodePoint`]'s `StringBuilder` was already doing a byte at a time
/// underneath.
fn writePoint(buffer: ByteBuffer, point: Int) {
  if point < 128 {
    appendUtf8Byte(buffer, point)
    return
  }
  if point < 2048 {
    let low = point / 64
    appendUtf8Byte(buffer, 192 + low)
    appendUtf8Byte(buffer, 128 + point - low * 64)
    return
  }
  if point < 65536 {
    let low = point / 64
    let lead = low / 64
    appendUtf8Byte(buffer, 224 + lead)
    appendUtf8Byte(buffer, 128 + low - lead * 64)
    appendUtf8Byte(buffer, 128 + point - low * 64)
    return
  }
  let low = point / 64
  let middle = low / 64
  let lead = middle / 64
  appendUtf8Byte(buffer, 240 + lead)
  appendUtf8Byte(buffer, 128 + middle - lead * 64)
  appendUtf8Byte(buffer, 128 + low - middle * 64)
  appendUtf8Byte(buffer, 128 + point - low * 64)
}

/// One byte onto the end of `buffer`: ADR 0062's ensure, store and commit.
///
/// `std.stringbuilder.appendByteInto` written again rather than shared,
/// because sharing it would mean `std.string` depending on `std.stringbuilder`
/// for three instructions — and because what makes this correct is the shape
/// and not the name: the index is the store's own length, read after the
/// `ensure`, and the count is the one the `ensure` was made for. That is the
/// only write a reservation admits.
fn appendUtf8Byte(buffer: ByteBuffer, value: Int) {
  let at = core.bytesLength(buffer)
  core.bytesEnsure(buffer, 1)
  core.bytesStore(buffer, at, value)
  core.bytesCommit(buffer, 1)
}

/// Unicode 17.0.0's full uppercase mapping, packed.
///
/// **302 records of thirteen base-32 symbols, 3,926 bytes**, covering 1,580
/// code points — 1,478 that map to one character and 102 that expand.
/// [`caseMappingOf`] is the reader and describes the record; [`packedField`]
/// describes the alphabet.
///
/// # It is generated, and a toolchain bump is a failing test
///
/// `crates/cove-sema/tests/unicase.rs` builds all six of these tables from
/// `char::to_uppercase`, `char::to_lowercase` and the observable behaviour of
/// the toolchain's own `Final_Sigma`, and asserts that what it built is byte
/// for byte what is written here. So this is not hand-maintained data: it is an
/// asset with a generator beside it, which is ADR 0064's Decision 5, and the
/// command that rewrites it is in that file's header.
///
/// A function rather than a constant because Cove has no module-level
/// constant. It costs nothing: this is one `Inst::Str`, and
/// [ADR 0045](../../../docs/adr/0045-a-literal-is-there-before-the-program-runs.md)
/// placed the object before the run's first instruction. A program that calls
/// neither [`toUpper`] nor [`toLower`] never lowers this function at all, so
/// it does not pay a byte for the table — measured, on `examples/covefmt`,
/// whose counters are identical across this change.
fn upperRuns() -> String {
  "000310J10?OO00005E0110@0G70006O01110002000700G10?OO00007H0710?OO00007O0110@03I000810H20?OOO0009A0110?OHH0009C0320?OOO0009J0820?OOO000:901110013000:;0G20?OOO000;J0320?OOO000;O0110?OFD000<00110@063000<30220?OOO000<80110?OOO000<<0110?OOO000<B0110?OOO000<E0110@031000<I0110?OOO000<J0110@053000<K0110A9B1000<N0110@042000=10320?OOO000=80110?OOO000==0110?OOO000=@0110?OOO000=D0220?OOO000=I0110?OOO000=M0110?OOO000=O0110@01H000>50110?OOO000>60110?OON000>80110?OOO000>90110?OON000>;0110?OOO000><0110?OON000>>0820?OOO000>M0110?OMA000>O0920?OOO000?@0111002C000?B0110?OOO000?C0110?OON000?E0110?OOO000?I0D20?OOO000A30920?OOO000AL0110?OOO000AO0210@:AO000B20110?OOO000B70520?OOO000B@0110@:@O000BA0110@:@L000BB0110@:@N000BC0110?OI>000BD0110?OIB000BF0210?OIC000BI0110?OIF000BK0110?OIE000BL0110A9:?000C00110?OIC000C10110A9:;000C30110?OIA000C40110A9;7000C50110A998000C60110A9:4000C80110?OI?000C90110?OI=000C:0110A9:4000C;0110@:?G000C<0110A9:1000C?0110?OI=000CA0110@:?M000CB0110?OI;000CE0110?OI:000CM0110@:?7000D00110?OI6000D20110A9:3000D30110?OI6000D70110A99:000D80110?OI6000D90110?OMK000D:0210?OI7000D<0110?OMI000DB0110?OI5000DM0110A98E000DN0110A98B000J50110@02D000KA0220?OOO000KG0110?OOO000KK0310@042000L@01110046000M<0110?ONJ000M=0310?ONK000M@01110076000MA0A10?OO0000N20110?OO1000N30910?OO0000N<0110?ON0000N=0210?ON1000N@0110?ON2000NA0110?ON7000NE0110?ONA000NF0110?ON:000NG0110?OOH000NI0<20?OOO000O@0110?OM:000OA0110?OM@000OB0110@007000OC0110?OL<000OE0110?OM0000OH0110?OOO000OK0110?OOO0011@1010?OO00012@0@10?OM@001310A20?OOO0014;0K20?OOO001620720?OOO0016?0110?OOA0016A1@20?OOO001;11610?ON@001<7011100:40046@1;10@2N00047M0310@2N0004OH0610?OOH007400110?ILB007410110?ILC007420110?ILL007430210?ILN007450110?ILM007460110?IM4007470110?INK007480110A2>20074:0110?OOO007;I0110A2@4007;M0110@3G6007<>0110A2AH007@12;20?OOO007DF011100<3007DG011100=C007DH011100?3007DI011100@C007DJ011100B3007DK0110?ON5007E11@20?OOO007H00810@008007H@0610@008007I00810@008007I@0810@008007J00610@008007J@011100CD007JA0110@008007JB011100EF007JC011